WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.510 --> 00:00:10.310
- Welcome everyone, and thank you so much

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.750
for joining us for the
Yale Development Dialogues,

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.100
a series convening economists, historians,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.260
and policymakers to discuss
some of the most pressing issues

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:19.990
confronting developing countries.

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:20.930
I'm Catherine Cheney.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:22.410
I'm a Senior Reporter at Devex,

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:23.730
and I'll be your moderator today,

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.850
as we ask the big question:

00:00:25.850 --> 00:00:28.980
Is it time to re-imagine
sustainable development?

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:30.360
So, as many of you know,

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:34.080
the 76th session of the United
Nations General Assembly

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.470
opens today.

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:36.920
And, leaders from around the world

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.240
will be meeting virtually and in person

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.500
to discuss progress on the
sustainable development goals.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:45.500
So as a reporter, focused
on technology and innovation

00:00:45.500 --> 00:00:47.214
in global health and
international development,

00:00:47.214 --> 00:00:49.310
I often refer to the SDGs,

00:00:49.310 --> 00:00:52.000
really as a way to capture
the issues that I cover.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.220
At Devex, our motto and
mission is do good, do it well.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:58.580
And we cover what is really a
global development industry.

00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:00.140
I see journalism as a way

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:02.720
to highlight what is
working and what isn't,

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.870
when it comes to making
progress on the SDGs,

00:01:05.870 --> 00:01:09.180
spreading the best ideas and
holding the sector accountable,

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:11.500
which I think will be
critical in making progress

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:13.100
on these ambitious goals.

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:15.850
But, today we actually ask:
Do these goals make sense?

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:18.836
Are they the best way to set ambitions,

00:01:18.836 --> 00:01:22.770
to measure progress, to
hold the world to account?

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:24.150
For those who are less familiar,

00:01:24.150 --> 00:01:25.780
and I'll just give a
little bit of background

00:01:25.780 --> 00:01:28.060
before our panelists expand on this,

00:01:28.060 --> 00:01:31.550
the SDGs are a list of 17
interlinked global goals.

00:01:31.550 --> 00:01:32.383
They were adopted

00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:35.100
by the United Nations
member states in 2015,

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:36.460
and they focus on everything

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:38.770
from tackling poverty and inequality,

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:40.830
to improving health and education,

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:44.120
issues like water, sanitation,
clean energy, climate action,

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.870
industry and infrastructure, decent work,

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:48.960
peace, justice, and strong institutions.

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:50.390
And finally, goal 17:

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.500
Partnerships to achieve all these goals.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.640
There are specific targets for each goal,

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.500
and indicators to measure progress,

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:58.800
with this deadline of 2030.

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:00.860
The SDGs serve, really as a blueprint,

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:03.000
for how to solve the
world's greatest challenges,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.260
but they're also the
subject of intense debate.

00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:07.510
And I actually think
the COVID-19 pandemic,

00:02:07.510 --> 00:02:10.283
among other things, has
led more people to ask:

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:12.860
How realistic are the SDGs?

00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:14.560
Do they measure the right things?

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:17.300
And, how can they really
hold up in the face of crises

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:19.930
like COVID-19 and climate change?

00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:22.000
So today, we're joined by
several experts on the issue.

00:02:22.000 --> 00:02:23.300
I'm excited to hear from them

00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.680
on exactly how we should
re-imagine sustainable development.

00:02:26.680 --> 00:02:28.430
I'll go ahead and introduce them.

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:31.500
Stefan Dercon is Professor
of Economic Policy

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.200
at the Blavatnik School of Government,

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:36.160
in the Economics Department
at the University of Oxford.

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.640
He's also Policy Advisor to Dominic Raab,

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.170
the UK Secretary of State

00:02:40.170 --> 00:02:42.870
for Foreign Commonwealth
and Development Affairs,

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:44.930
and First Secretary of State.

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:48.610
Between 2011 and 2017, he
was the Chief Economist

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:50.804
of the Department of
International Development,

00:02:50.804 --> 00:02:52.830
where Rory Stewart was as well.

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:54.380
You'll hear about him in a moment.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.210
We're also joined by Shanta Devarajan.

00:02:57.210 --> 00:02:59.180
He's Professor of the
Practice of Development,

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:00.510
at Georgetown University's

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.850
Edmund A. Walsh School of Foreign Service.

00:03:02.850 --> 00:03:04.990
And his previous positions
include Senior Director

00:03:04.990 --> 00:03:06.310
for Development Economics,

00:03:06.310 --> 00:03:09.190
and former Acting Chief Economist
at the World Bank Group.

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:12.210
He was a Director of the
"World Development Report 2004,

00:03:12.210 --> 00:03:14.480
Making Services Work for Poor People"

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.800
And, I'm sure he'll draw
on some of those insights.

00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:18.210
And finally, Rory Stewart,

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:19.879
one of the three Co-Organizers

00:03:19.879 --> 00:03:21.740
of the Yale Development Dialogues,

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:22.840
is a Senior Fellow

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:25.080
at Yale Jackson Institute
for Global Affairs,

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:27.620
where he focuses on
contemporary politics and crisis

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:29.110
in international development,

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:31.870
and intervention in fragile
and conflict-affected states.

00:03:31.870 --> 00:03:33.930
Lately, he's been very
busy with Afghanistan,

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:35.310
as you might imagine.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.450
He was previously the
UK Secretary of State

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.680
for International Development.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.820
And in that role, he
doubled the UK's investment

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.474
in climate and the environment.

00:03:42.474 --> 00:03:43.880
And, the three of these panelists

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.270
have worked very closely
together over the years.

00:03:46.270 --> 00:03:48.500
And, as one of the organizers

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:49.900
of the Yale Development Dialogues,

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:52.290
Rory has really taken the
lead on framing the session

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.500
and saying, "This is why we
need to have this conversation.

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:56.572
And this is why, now."

00:03:56.572 --> 00:03:59.130
So before I go to Rory,
I just want to note,

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:00.820
we really love to hear from our audience.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:02.930
We always have great
questions from you all.

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:05.400
So, while I definitely have
some questions prepared,

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:06.590
please throw your questions

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:08.560
into the Q and A function on Zoom,

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.750
and I'll get to as many as I can.

00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:13.440
But Rory, I do want to
start with you here.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:14.570
This big question:

00:04:14.570 --> 00:04:16.580
Is it time to re-imagine
sustainable development?

00:04:16.580 --> 00:04:19.770
This really came from you, and you know,

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:23.330
I think you picked the perfect
people in Stefan and Shanta

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:24.660
to discuss this big question.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.030
So, can you expand on why
this question? Why now?

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:30.630
Why is it so critical we
have this discussion today?

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.020
- I think the first thing,

00:04:33.020 --> 00:04:34.450
thank you all very much for having me.

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:36.670
And, I obviously want to spend more time

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:38.730
giving you all the time
speak, rather than myself.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:42.760
But, I think one thing
as an ex-politician,

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:44.020
which is important to frame,

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.183
is the ways in which things
like the SDGs emerge.

00:04:48.490 --> 00:04:49.850
It isn't simply

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:52.030
that very distinguished
development economists,

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:54.760
like Shanta or Stefan,
sit down and try to think

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.620
in the most clear sighted, logical way

00:04:57.620 --> 00:05:00.040
about the most efficient
research they've done

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:03.113
on how to do development well.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.390
What, in fact, happens
with the SDGs, of course,

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.240
is that it is primarily a
response to another set of goals

00:05:09.240 --> 00:05:10.760
called the Millennium Development Goals,

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:13.067
that came about in 2000.

00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:16.550
And which, by 2015, we're
looking a little bit tired.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:17.410
So in 2015,

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.510
there was another pump to
get everything going again,

00:05:19.510 --> 00:05:22.769
this time with a target of 2030.

00:05:22.769 --> 00:05:26.560
And, underlying an enormous
amount of what's going on

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.166
is a funny combination of
academic literature and research.

00:05:31.166 --> 00:05:31.999
Right?

00:05:31.999 --> 00:05:33.280
Which is transforming these goals

00:05:33.280 --> 00:05:35.122
and taking them from the aid goals

00:05:35.122 --> 00:05:36.640
with the Millennium Development Goals,

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:39.220
up to 17, and new things emerging.

00:05:39.220 --> 00:05:41.000
If you dig under the surface of it,

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:42.390
a lot of what's happening in the SDGs

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:45.270
is this, sort of, critique of the MDGs.

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.970
So, one big change is suddenly
these goals become universal.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:51.623
They relate not just to
developing countries,

00:05:51.623 --> 00:05:53.143
they're to relate all the
countries of the world.

00:05:53.143 --> 00:05:55.070
So, the United States, United Kingdom

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:56.920
is supposed to be signing up

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:59.253
to pursue these same common goals.

00:05:59.253 --> 00:06:02.184
Much more emphasis on
things like peace building,

00:06:02.184 --> 00:06:04.080
governance, human rights.

00:06:04.080 --> 00:06:06.810
Much more emphasis on the private sector.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:09.973
Much more evidence on
biodiversity on land and sea.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:13.120
Why did these changes happen in 2015?

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:15.427
Well, because people obviously
felt two things there.

00:06:15.427 --> 00:06:17.620
One of them is, here are
things which are excluded,

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:19.278
that we care about, like biodiversity.

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:22.740
Second thing, of course,
there is a political pushback

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:23.573
from people saying,

00:06:23.573 --> 00:06:26.160
this shouldn't simply be
countries in the north

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:27.830
telling countries in south how to behave.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.590
We should all be trying
to pursue these goals.

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.510
So, become more universal,
become more comprehensive,

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:36.670
also become far more ambitious.

00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:39.930
Suddenly there is a commitment
not to halving things

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:40.920
like poverty and hunger.

00:06:40.920 --> 00:06:44.000
There's commitments to getting
to zero on these things.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.920
And, new forms of measurement come in

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:49.507
to try to guess how effective they are.

00:06:49.507 --> 00:06:51.129
And I, as a working politician,

00:06:51.129 --> 00:06:54.180
found this absolutely
central to what I was doing.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.190
So, I would turn up at
the UN General Assembly.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:58.727
I did a big session as
the Secretary of State

00:06:58.727 --> 00:07:02.040
on the Sustainable Development
Goals, gave a speech on it,

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:03.940
run a whole series of different events,

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:05.970
UN General Assembly around them.

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:07.300
And we all talk, sovereignly,

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.900
about those things within
them, which we liked

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:11.900
that for me, as a politician,

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.140
obviously I was particularly excited

00:07:14.140 --> 00:07:17.270
at the emphasis on
climate and biodiversity.

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:19.270
So, I put a huge emphasis
on that when I spoke.

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:21.830
Other people are very
interested in the shift,

00:07:21.830 --> 00:07:23.100
just from talking about education,

00:07:23.100 --> 00:07:25.840
talking about the quality
of education, right?

00:07:25.840 --> 00:07:27.940
Other people are extremely excited that,

00:07:27.940 --> 00:07:29.040
really, it allows them

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:32.184
to get into a (indistinct)
governance, human rights,

00:07:32.184 --> 00:07:33.993
in a much more direct way.

00:07:35.090 --> 00:07:36.723
But somewhere in the background,

00:07:38.216 --> 00:07:40.940
you know, Professor Devarajan
and Professor Dercon

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:42.620
sitting there, looking
at this, and saying,

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:44.480
out of all this mess, of all these things,

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:48.500
generated by politicians, by
citizens, by lobby groups,

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:51.792
by NGOs, all of them are
feeding into this process,

00:07:51.792 --> 00:07:53.620
what is getting lost?

00:07:53.620 --> 00:07:56.850
What's been excluded from this, right?

00:07:56.850 --> 00:07:57.810
Have we lost sight?

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:02.810
For example: As Stefan might
say, on a focus on poverty,

00:08:03.330 --> 00:08:05.720
or have we lost sight on
effects on economic growth,

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:07.480
or if we lost sight on a focus

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:10.940
of actually what works
and what doesn't work?

00:08:10.940 --> 00:08:12.120
Because I think, and it's on this

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:14.270
that I'm gonna hand over
to the other panelists,

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:16.300
I think somewhere within this, of course,

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:19.760
the great fundamental point,
which is that (indistinct).

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:22.210
You don't have a moral obligation
to do what you can't do.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:23.970
And therefore, the fundamental question,

00:08:23.970 --> 00:08:27.710
of what works and what doesn't
work, somehow concealed

00:08:27.710 --> 00:08:30.350
in a series of incredibly
idealistic statements

00:08:30.350 --> 00:08:32.275
about how we would like the world to be.

00:08:32.275 --> 00:08:33.525
And, on that I hand over.

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:35.352
- Thank you, Rory.

00:08:35.352 --> 00:08:36.600
Stefan, I do want to bring you into this.

00:08:36.600 --> 00:08:40.070
And first of all, I know you,

00:08:40.070 --> 00:08:41.220
as we critique the goals,

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:43.470
because I do want to start with a critique

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:46.220
because before we propose solutions,

00:08:46.220 --> 00:08:48.550
we need to understand the problem here.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:51.750
I know one, the goals are
idealistic, as Rory said.

00:08:51.750 --> 00:08:53.320
And would love for you to expand on that.

00:08:53.320 --> 00:08:56.060
And two, in terms of who
they are relevant for.

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:59.620
You know, Rory mentioned as
a politician from the UK,

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:01.400
going to the UN General Assembly,

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:03.410
this was a useful blueprint.

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:04.920
It served a purpose,

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.240
and he was able to use
these goals as a framework,

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:10.162
but perhaps, not so relevant
or useful for everyone.

00:09:10.162 --> 00:09:13.000
So can you jump in on your experience,

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:15.700
in terms of, how the goals have emerged

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.100
and whether they make sense?

00:09:18.420 --> 00:09:20.060
- Well, thank you very much.

00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:20.893
And, look.

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:23.910
What is there to dislike
about as the SDGs?

00:09:23.910 --> 00:09:25.340
You know, we should only celebrate them.

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:28.510
You know, we have zero poverty
in there and zero hunger.

00:09:28.510 --> 00:09:30.260
Poverty is still there (indistinct) G1.

00:09:30.260 --> 00:09:31.890
We have learning for all.

00:09:31.890 --> 00:09:33.590
We have economic opportunities for all.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:35.990
But also, lots more attention to planet,

00:09:35.990 --> 00:09:39.377
to biodiversity, to
climate issues, and so on.

00:09:39.377 --> 00:09:43.200
You know, we have there,
something quite remarkable.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.430
Kind of a vision for Sweden
in the whole world, you know?

00:09:46.430 --> 00:09:49.290
A beautiful blueprint for the world.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:52.310
And we got all the leaders in
the world to sign up for this.

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:54.590
What more? Actually the job is done.

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:55.690
You know, maybe we're there.

00:09:55.690 --> 00:09:58.427
And this is a bit like, well,

00:09:58.427 --> 00:10:01.683
I find it actually really
quite problematic, okay?

00:10:01.683 --> 00:10:05.190
There's a number of things
that fundamentally get lost

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:07.400
by celebrating and only celebrating,

00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:09.640
and the way we talk about
and the way to do it.

00:10:09.640 --> 00:10:12.160
So, it's not that I'm against the SDGs.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:15.250
I think, indeed, it's
about their relevance,

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:16.640
and what we've actually lost.

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.210
So for me, maybe three points to make.

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:21.190
So the first one is, you know,

00:10:21.190 --> 00:10:24.360
Rory mentioned the
Millennium Development Goals.

00:10:24.360 --> 00:10:25.690
The Millennium Development Goals,

00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:28.770
they came out of the Millennium Summit.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.580
And there's this famous photograph,

00:10:30.580 --> 00:10:33.500
probably the only time
in the global history

00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:34.670
that so many leaders in the world

00:10:34.670 --> 00:10:36.940
were on the same photograph.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:39.690
I counted them. 149 Heads of States.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.140
It was signed by 189 leaders.

00:10:42.120 --> 00:10:43.587
They signed up to these MDGs,

00:10:43.587 --> 00:10:45.530
and indeed, Rory said
they weren't perfect,

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:48.540
but they were the beginning,
and then the SDGs were there.

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:50.760
Now, I have a problem with that photograph

00:10:50.760 --> 00:10:51.593
because I look at that,

00:10:51.593 --> 00:10:54.250
and yes, I see there are,
you know, decent people.

00:10:54.250 --> 00:10:56.280
I see Kofi Annan there, I see Tony Blair,

00:10:56.280 --> 00:10:58.110
I see Bill Clinton, Jacques Chirac,

00:10:58.110 --> 00:10:59.730
and maybe I don't agree with all of them.

00:10:59.730 --> 00:11:00.563
It's there.

00:11:00.563 --> 00:11:05.060
You see Jiang Zemin from China,
and see Putin there as well.

00:11:05.060 --> 00:11:07.200
But actually, you have
to start looking further.

00:11:07.200 --> 00:11:09.960
And then, you see people
that have been put in front

00:11:09.960 --> 00:11:13.040
of the International Court of Justice.

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:15.230
You have incredible groups

00:11:15.230 --> 00:11:17.270
that came to power with massive violence,

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:20.130
and continue to perpetrate
massive violence.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:24.020
You have terribly corrupt
leaders there as well, and so on.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:24.960
And then, you ask yourself:

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:26.940
Well, what's the point of signing up

00:11:26.940 --> 00:11:28.710
to the SDGs or the MDGs?

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.140
What's the point?

00:11:30.140 --> 00:11:32.230
Of course, to those
who are decent leaders,

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:34.210
and they want growth and
development in their country.

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:36.800
Of course, no country's perfect.

00:11:36.800 --> 00:11:38.810
There is no Sweden in the world, really,

00:11:38.810 --> 00:11:41.070
in that kind of idealized way.

00:11:41.070 --> 00:11:42.610
But for them, it's just fine.

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:44.660
It's business as usual.
This is what we try to do.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:47.080
And especially with the
SDGs, we can pick and choose.

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:48.500
And so, there's enough for us.

00:11:48.500 --> 00:11:50.320
We can do something. It's good.

00:11:50.320 --> 00:11:51.960
But, it doesn't make any difference.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.560
Not for the countries where
it really would matter,

00:11:54.560 --> 00:11:56.470
where poverty is terrible,

00:11:56.470 --> 00:11:58.410
where economic opportunities are dreadful,

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.250
where there's no learning for all,

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:03.450
where there is a terrible
attention to biodiversity.

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:04.680
Who cares?

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:07.290
Nobody needs to, is
held to account for it.

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:08.123
You know,

00:12:08.123 --> 00:12:10.550
calling them out on the
SDGs doesn't mean anything.

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:11.827
So it's basically, you know,

00:12:11.827 --> 00:12:15.070
for the countries for
where it would matter most,

00:12:15.070 --> 00:12:16.350
it doesn't do anything.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:18.137
Now, we can say maybe (indistinct).

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:21.990
And maybe, the second point
is we should just be conscious

00:12:21.990 --> 00:12:24.290
that it's probably, in the end,

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:26.830
this is really in the
end for rich countries.

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:28.350
This is basically rich countries

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:30.560
to have have an accountability framework

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:32.130
for what they do in the world.

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:35.393
So indeed, I know that
definitely for the MDGs and SDGs.

00:12:35.393 --> 00:12:39.080
You know, where Rory was my
boss in the Secretary of State.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:42.040
It mattered for DIFD at
the time. It mattered.

00:12:42.040 --> 00:12:43.127
You would look at it and go,

00:12:43.127 --> 00:12:44.717
"Are we doing what we doing?" And, so on.

00:12:44.717 --> 00:12:46.730
So, maybe that's not so bad.

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:48.660
And actually, we can attest to that.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:51.160
It focused more on
outcomes, than on problems.

00:12:51.160 --> 00:12:52.930
But again, for whom is it relevant?

00:12:52.930 --> 00:12:55.000
Does it really matter for
those who, fundamentally,

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.011
we should be even care about?

00:12:57.011 --> 00:12:58.180
It leads me to the final thing,

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.054
and that's actually where it
really gets problematic for me.

00:13:01.054 --> 00:13:04.120
We see, then, the UN sitting
out, we have the SDGs.

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:06.560
Now it's going to cost
you five to seven trillion

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:07.820
to achieve them.

00:13:07.820 --> 00:13:09.790
And every time again, that's what we hear.

00:13:09.790 --> 00:13:11.260
And, it's going to cost us that much,

00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:13.562
as if there's a supermarket for SDGs,

00:13:13.562 --> 00:13:15.650
where we can go and buy them,

00:13:15.650 --> 00:13:18.460
and then achieve our results in the world.

00:13:18.460 --> 00:13:19.800
Now there isn't that supermarket.

00:13:19.800 --> 00:13:23.110
In fact, you know, people
may think it's calculations.

00:13:23.110 --> 00:13:26.299
In fact, Shanta, you may want
to defend yourself later,

00:13:26.299 --> 00:13:28.510
did one of the first calculations

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:29.627
of how much it was for the MPGs.

00:13:29.627 --> 00:13:30.977
So, you know, you have disowned it.

00:13:30.977 --> 00:13:33.480
You know, you have made
your original sin there

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.860
and you've clearly put it aside now,

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:37.750
and you've confessed your sins.

00:13:37.750 --> 00:13:38.630
But, it's fine.

00:13:38.630 --> 00:13:40.290
But you know, you get this impression

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:42.150
that that's what you can do.

00:13:42.150 --> 00:13:44.020
And actually, if we go
back to the countries

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:45.970
where it should matter most,

00:13:45.970 --> 00:13:47.920
it's not money that's the problem.

00:13:47.920 --> 00:13:50.323
It's actually fundamentally, you know,

00:13:50.323 --> 00:13:53.540
groups that are in power,
economic and political deals

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:57.890
that are entirely focused on
rent-sharing for themselves.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:01.595
You know, as Shanta himself
is quoted in a blog to say,

00:14:01.595 --> 00:14:04.760
you know, what would it cost
to get development in the DRC?

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:05.750
Probably infinity.

00:14:05.750 --> 00:14:08.512
Because there's no amount
of money that you can spend

00:14:08.512 --> 00:14:12.320
at a time on President
Kabila, who wasn't interested

00:14:12.320 --> 00:14:13.930
in growth and development (indistinct).

00:14:13.930 --> 00:14:15.780
He was only interested in computer games

00:14:15.780 --> 00:14:17.930
and making a lot of money.

00:14:17.930 --> 00:14:19.640
You know, you can spend whatever you want.

00:14:19.640 --> 00:14:20.510
It doesn't make progress.

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:22.200
So what's the relevance,

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:25.199
if we focus on the wrong countries,

00:14:25.199 --> 00:14:28.910
and actually keep them
focusing just on development,

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:30.480
as just something you can buy?

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:33.730
And it's just about spending
massive amounts of money

00:14:33.730 --> 00:14:35.420
and that's all that counts.

00:14:35.420 --> 00:14:36.940
I'll stop now.

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:37.773
- Thank you, Stefan.

00:14:37.773 --> 00:14:39.270
And perfect transition to Shanta.

00:14:39.270 --> 00:14:41.190
And I will say, Shanta,

00:14:41.190 --> 00:14:45.038
I know you have been quite
upfront about, you know,

00:14:45.038 --> 00:14:47.760
the challenge of putting
a price tag on this.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:50.950
In fact, in preparation
for this conversation,

00:14:50.950 --> 00:14:52.457
I read a blog post you wrote,

00:14:52.457 --> 00:14:55.940
"Shame on me: Why it was
wrong to cost the MDGs."

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:58.350
And here we are, doing
the same for the SDGs,

00:14:58.350 --> 00:15:00.140
despite the challenges
that Stefan outlined.

00:15:00.140 --> 00:15:01.500
So, can you expand on this a little bit,

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.120
when it comes to the problems with SDGs,

00:15:04.120 --> 00:15:06.520
why is this costing issue
part of the problem?

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:09.250
And perhaps, you have more to add to that.

00:15:09.250 --> 00:15:10.113
- Sure. Well, thank you very much.

00:15:10.113 --> 00:15:12.130
This is a real pleasure and an honor

00:15:12.130 --> 00:15:14.233
to be in this (indistinct) group.

00:15:15.600 --> 00:15:17.110
You know, the problem is,

00:15:17.110 --> 00:15:19.920
actually, if we go back to the
Millennium Development Goals

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:23.293
that both Rory and Stefan mentioned.

00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:28.104
Let's be honest, they were
essentially an attempt

00:15:28.104 --> 00:15:32.163
to raise funds for foreign aid.

00:15:33.402 --> 00:15:37.237
And basically, the taxpayers
in rich countries were asking,

00:15:37.237 --> 00:15:40.900
"What are we paying for
all of this foreign aid?"

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:43.560
And they came out with
a set of goals saying,

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:45.540
we're trying to reduce child mortality

00:15:45.540 --> 00:15:49.550
and increase primary
enrollment, and so on.

00:15:49.550 --> 00:15:53.830
And in a narrow sense, it worked,

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:56.907
It did raise foreign aid, since,

00:15:56.907 --> 00:16:00.313
and almost doubled foreign
aid during that period.

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:06.200
The problem is that we got
trapped into the situation,

00:16:07.820 --> 00:16:12.240
which Stefan alluded to,
thinking that's all it took,

00:16:12.240 --> 00:16:13.550
was to get the foreign aid.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:14.850
And then we would be done,

00:16:16.150 --> 00:16:21.150
which was the point I was trying
to make in that blog post.

00:16:21.330 --> 00:16:23.580
But, I think it's
actually worse than that.

00:16:24.600 --> 00:16:27.270
By focusing on the money,

00:16:27.270 --> 00:16:32.200
it takes away from what
is the critical component,

00:16:32.200 --> 00:16:35.210
which is the accountability
of these governments

00:16:35.210 --> 00:16:36.463
to their own people.

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:41.538
The problem is with, if you
say, "Here's $50 billion",

00:16:41.538 --> 00:16:43.600
which was our estimate for the MDGs,

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:45.150
and now it's five trillion.

00:16:45.150 --> 00:16:47.980
Was it, for the SDGs?

00:16:47.980 --> 00:16:50.290
If you say, well, that's
what it's going to take,

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:53.340
then there's no attempt
for the Heads of State

00:16:53.340 --> 00:16:55.817
to actually tell their people,

00:16:55.817 --> 00:16:58.680
"Oh, we have to make all
these tough decisions",

00:16:58.680 --> 00:17:01.700
because the reason we are not
making progress on the SDGs

00:17:01.700 --> 00:17:04.340
is that they're not
coming up with the money.

00:17:04.340 --> 00:17:07.020
So, it gets governments off the hook,

00:17:07.020 --> 00:17:10.450
rather than actually being
able to hold them accountable

00:17:10.450 --> 00:17:13.290
for delivering on these goals.

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:18.290
So I think the focus on
the billions to trillions

00:17:19.210 --> 00:17:23.680
is actually doing harm, not
even if that it's misguided,

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:25.590
not just that it's misguided.

00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:29.630
But let me say on a positive note,

00:17:29.630 --> 00:17:32.130
the other thing is that we
have learned quite a bit

00:17:32.130 --> 00:17:37.060
about how to make public spending
effective over the years.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:39.437
So the other problem, of course, was that,

00:17:39.437 --> 00:17:40.840
if you just had the money,

00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:42.780
it doesn't mean you're
going to get to these goals.

00:17:42.780 --> 00:17:43.770
Oh yeah, and a lot of people

00:17:43.770 --> 00:17:45.286
have been doing very important research

00:17:45.286 --> 00:17:49.130
on how to deliver on public services.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:49.963
And there,

00:17:49.963 --> 00:17:52.869
I would say that I'd like to
see more effort by governments

00:17:52.869 --> 00:17:55.020
to work with communities.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:57.280
There's quite a lot of
rigorous evidence now

00:17:57.280 --> 00:18:01.356
that actually having communities
monitor service providers,

00:18:01.356 --> 00:18:04.070
can actually lead to better outcomes,

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:06.962
like reduced child mortality.

00:18:06.962 --> 00:18:11.470
Transparency of information
is another critical component.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:14.680
When people know what it is
that they're supposed to get

00:18:15.590 --> 00:18:17.910
and what it is they're actually getting,

00:18:17.910 --> 00:18:22.643
they mobilize and they
start demanding efforts.

00:18:23.654 --> 00:18:26.070
And let's be very frank, different ways

00:18:26.070 --> 00:18:31.070
of avoiding the political
capture of public finance.

00:18:31.510 --> 00:18:33.680
That's the problem we have.

00:18:33.680 --> 00:18:36.780
Stefan alluded to DR Congo,

00:18:36.780 --> 00:18:39.180
but we have that problem
everywhere, including,

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:44.180
to some extent, in the United
States of political capture.

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:48.760
Now, there are societies that
have tried to get around it,

00:18:48.760 --> 00:18:51.114
including things like
using electronic transfer

00:18:51.114 --> 00:18:56.114
of social protection payments
that make it much harder,

00:18:56.260 --> 00:18:57.310
it doesn't eliminate it,

00:18:57.310 --> 00:18:59.820
but it makes it much harder
for people to steal it

00:18:59.820 --> 00:19:03.240
before it gets to the
intended beneficiary.

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:06.051
So we have a big agenda here of,

00:19:06.051 --> 00:19:10.580
let's put the stage
SDGs aside for a minute,

00:19:10.580 --> 00:19:14.749
and say, how do we actually
help improve people's lives?

00:19:14.749 --> 00:19:18.080
And I think, the agenda is critical today

00:19:18.080 --> 00:19:20.880
because we also have this COVID crisis.

00:19:20.880 --> 00:19:22.890
And in the middle of the COVID crisis,

00:19:22.890 --> 00:19:25.880
there's been a big push to
increase public spending

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:28.680
on health, on education
and social protection.

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:30.010
All for very good reasons.

00:19:30.010 --> 00:19:34.040
There's no question that
you need more resources,

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:37.910
but if we spend it the way we
been spending it in the past,

00:19:37.910 --> 00:19:40.010
we're not going to solve the COVID crisis,

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:43.240
and that crisis can blow up.

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:46.010
So I don't wanna say, let's-

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:49.260
For the COVID crisis itself,
let's use what we've learned

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:52.030
about increasing effectiveness
of public spending.

00:19:52.030 --> 00:19:55.030
But more generally for the SDGs,

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:58.340
let's drop the discussion of trillions

00:19:58.340 --> 00:20:00.193
and let's talk about effectiveness.

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:03.090
- Thank you for that.
Rory, you want to jump in?

00:20:03.090 --> 00:20:03.923
- Yeah.

00:20:03.923 --> 00:20:06.380
So, just to try to draw some
of these themes together.

00:20:06.380 --> 00:20:07.440
So I think,

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:11.360
that the first thing is
somewhat underlying the way

00:20:11.360 --> 00:20:13.350
in which we think about
not just development,

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:15.790
but almost everything
we do in our society,

00:20:15.790 --> 00:20:18.333
is we have an idea that
somehow we have a plan,

00:20:19.386 --> 00:20:21.550
a some sort of template,

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:24.330
and if we just put enough
money and resources behind it,

00:20:24.330 --> 00:20:26.150
we can sort it out.

00:20:26.150 --> 00:20:27.820
And, we're particularly prone to this idea

00:20:27.820 --> 00:20:30.410
when we're dealing with
somebody else's country.

00:20:30.410 --> 00:20:33.220
If you actually were
charged with trying to work

00:20:33.220 --> 00:20:37.140
with a ex coal community in East Kentucky,

00:20:37.140 --> 00:20:39.330
you might be a little bit
more cautious about thinking

00:20:39.330 --> 00:20:41.480
that you had some universal
template and plan,

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:43.010
and you were just going to come in,

00:20:43.010 --> 00:20:44.320
and if you put enough money behind it,

00:20:44.320 --> 00:20:46.240
the whole thing will be sorted out.

00:20:46.240 --> 00:20:49.280
Now, why is it we have this thought?

00:20:49.280 --> 00:20:51.564
Well, it's partly because
we find it very difficult,

00:20:51.564 --> 00:20:54.070
politically, and this is me
speaking as a politician,

00:20:54.070 --> 00:20:55.830
to talk publicly

00:20:55.830 --> 00:20:59.760
about how difficult
development progress is.

00:20:59.760 --> 00:21:01.000
But the reality is, of course,

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:03.550
that all of these programs
involve partial failures,

00:21:03.550 --> 00:21:07.230
partial success, two steps
forward, one step back.

00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:08.683
Many things don't work out.

00:21:09.930 --> 00:21:11.840
But, we don't want to
say that to the public.

00:21:11.840 --> 00:21:14.197
What we want to say to the public is,

00:21:14.197 --> 00:21:16.460
we have a plan, this is
what we're going to do,

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:18.400
this is this beautiful utopian vision,

00:21:18.400 --> 00:21:20.800
and if you just give us enough
money, we'll get behind it.

00:21:20.800 --> 00:21:23.560
And, it's particularly
strange in the US context,

00:21:23.560 --> 00:21:26.640
because in the US context, the marketing.

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:28.270
So Stefan talked about this idea

00:21:28.270 --> 00:21:30.550
about development being like a supermarket

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:33.200
where you turn up, and for a
few trillion, buy something.

00:21:33.200 --> 00:21:34.770
But, the other dimension,

00:21:34.770 --> 00:21:36.800
that Stefan maybe hasn't
emphasized enough,

00:21:36.800 --> 00:21:39.280
is the marketing of the product
in the supermarket, right?

00:21:39.280 --> 00:21:40.463
What's above the shelf?

00:21:41.350 --> 00:21:43.130
And in the case of the US,

00:21:43.130 --> 00:21:45.600
right from the very beginning
of the development project,

00:21:45.600 --> 00:21:48.040
under Truman in the 1940s,

00:21:48.040 --> 00:21:52.210
the marketing has been about
scaring the American people.

00:21:52.210 --> 00:21:53.391
Right?

00:21:53.391 --> 00:21:54.930
He actually says, when
he's launching Point Four,

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:55.857
a Congressman says to him,

00:21:55.857 --> 00:21:57.970
"We've got to scare the
hell out of people."

00:21:57.970 --> 00:22:00.070
So, it's all framed in terms of communism.

00:22:01.090 --> 00:22:03.460
And an enormous amount that then follows,

00:22:03.460 --> 00:22:04.920
in terms of US development spend.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:06.842
And of course, a lot
of the drivers of this,

00:22:06.842 --> 00:22:08.680
in the 40s and 50s, it's
not European countries,

00:22:08.680 --> 00:22:10.150
it's the United States.

00:22:10.150 --> 00:22:12.090
It's arranged around this fear.

00:22:12.090 --> 00:22:14.400
And, we had the same phenomenon
in Afghanistan recently

00:22:14.400 --> 00:22:16.804
around counter-terrorism, right?

00:22:16.804 --> 00:22:20.630
And, it is reinforced by
desire to have a simple mission

00:22:20.630 --> 00:22:22.835
that you can explain to the public.

00:22:22.835 --> 00:22:24.097
Now, what's the problem with that?

00:22:24.097 --> 00:22:25.450
The problem with that is that

00:22:25.450 --> 00:22:28.200
if you arrange all your programs
around this sort of simple,

00:22:28.200 --> 00:22:29.230
rather targeted mission,

00:22:29.230 --> 00:22:31.220
which markets well, which sells well,

00:22:31.220 --> 00:22:34.250
which, as Shanta says, is a
good way of getting people

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:36.570
to reach into their pockets,

00:22:36.570 --> 00:22:39.370
as soon as that narrative begins to fray,

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:40.370
there is a real risk

00:22:40.370 --> 00:22:41.840
that you're going to abandon everything.

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:43.280
So obviously, we've just seen this

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:44.530
with President Biden and Afghanistan.

00:22:44.530 --> 00:22:46.730
As soon as people are no longer confident

00:22:46.730 --> 00:22:48.110
that there are terrorists in Afghanistan,

00:22:48.110 --> 00:22:49.330
suddenly we're not going
to do any developments

00:22:49.330 --> 00:22:50.330
in Afghanistan, right?

00:22:50.330 --> 00:22:51.460
We hand the thing over to the Taliban,

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:52.530
we leave the country entirely,

00:22:52.530 --> 00:22:54.230
we're going to impose sanctions,

00:22:54.230 --> 00:22:56.300
and 14 million Afghans end up being much,

00:22:56.300 --> 00:22:57.793
much worse off now for it.

00:22:59.500 --> 00:23:01.400
What is the alternative to that?

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:02.770
Well, to loop back to where we began,

00:23:02.770 --> 00:23:04.730
the alternative would have to be

00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:07.490
to work out how to produce something,

00:23:07.490 --> 00:23:10.200
which is neither the extreme optimism

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:11.740
of the SDGs and the MDGs,

00:23:11.740 --> 00:23:14.040
these sort of incredible,
utopian statements

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:15.210
about plants, money,

00:23:15.210 --> 00:23:18.577
nor a total grim, pessimistic,

00:23:18.577 --> 00:23:21.660
"We can't do anything. The
whole world is impossible",

00:23:21.660 --> 00:23:24.790
but actually begin to
articulate imaginatively

00:23:24.790 --> 00:23:26.920
and practically what it would mean to work

00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:29.170
in different countries
in different contexts.

00:23:30.290 --> 00:23:33.530
Alright, what does it mean
to work in Rwanda, right,

00:23:33.530 --> 00:23:34.880
which is a development success story

00:23:34.880 --> 00:23:37.140
that has serious human rights problems?

00:23:37.140 --> 00:23:38.570
What does it mean to work in Ethiopia,

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:40.870
which seemed to be a
development success story

00:23:40.870 --> 00:23:42.160
with serious human rights problems,

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:43.640
and now looks very different

00:23:43.640 --> 00:23:46.140
to what it looked like
three years ago, right?

00:23:46.140 --> 00:23:48.740
What does it feel like to work in Ghana,

00:23:48.740 --> 00:23:51.110
which some development economists
don't want you to work in,

00:23:51.110 --> 00:23:52.780
because it's approaching
middle-income status

00:23:52.780 --> 00:23:54.710
and it's no longer poor enough?

00:23:54.710 --> 00:23:57.110
What does it feel like
to work in Pakistan,

00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:59.920
where actually international
aid has really leveraged

00:23:59.920 --> 00:24:01.680
because Pakistan's too big?

00:24:01.680 --> 00:24:03.423
It's economy's too large
for Pakistani politicians

00:24:03.423 --> 00:24:04.980
to want to pay much attention

00:24:04.980 --> 00:24:07.220
to what any outsider is saying.

00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:09.110
What does it feel like
to work in South Sudan,

00:24:09.110 --> 00:24:11.667
where Salva Kiir effectively
is prepared to turn around

00:24:11.667 --> 00:24:13.557
to the international community and say,

00:24:13.557 --> 00:24:14.780
"Do you want to cut off the aid?

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:16.730
I don't mind because the
people who are going to suffer

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:18.710
are just the people in the refugee camps,

00:24:18.710 --> 00:24:19.543
and the connected schools.

00:24:19.543 --> 00:24:21.690
I'm not interested in
this project at all."

00:24:21.690 --> 00:24:24.120
So it's producing something,

00:24:24.120 --> 00:24:27.790
which is much more located
in a place and a time,

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:29.350
and is much more located in questions

00:24:29.350 --> 00:24:31.627
of local power and politics.

00:24:31.627 --> 00:24:35.570
In other words, to, to quote
my favorite World Bank Report,

00:24:35.570 --> 00:24:38.230
produced four, five years ago,

00:24:38.230 --> 00:24:40.960
the entire framework that
underlies that switch is,

00:24:40.960 --> 00:24:42.880
that somewhere there is a best practice,

00:24:42.880 --> 00:24:44.850
and all we need to do
is do capacity-building

00:24:44.850 --> 00:24:46.420
and find political will,

00:24:46.420 --> 00:24:49.090
is deeply, deeply misleading, right?

00:24:49.090 --> 00:24:52.864
In the end, all of this
comes down to local context,

00:24:52.864 --> 00:24:56.720
local power and local politics,
with a capital P, right?

00:24:56.720 --> 00:24:58.510
Over the others.

00:24:58.510 --> 00:24:59.343
- Thank you, Rory.

00:24:59.343 --> 00:25:00.970
I mean, I think that is so well put.

00:25:00.970 --> 00:25:03.780
And I'm typing as you go
because it just helps reframe

00:25:03.780 --> 00:25:06.170
how I look at these issues, as a reporter.

00:25:06.170 --> 00:25:08.560
You know, on the one hand,

00:25:08.560 --> 00:25:11.710
Stefan has mentioned
that the SDGs are a bit

00:25:11.710 --> 00:25:14.430
like a blueprint for building
Sweden all over the world.

00:25:14.430 --> 00:25:18.000
And so, it's hard to critique
that idealistic vision.

00:25:18.000 --> 00:25:20.340
And yet, that is problematic

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:21.740
for the reasons Rory just outlined,

00:25:21.740 --> 00:25:23.770
that it really isn't just

00:25:23.770 --> 00:25:26.260
that we have best practices
we can replicate globally.

00:25:26.260 --> 00:25:28.603
Every place is different, context matters.

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.230
And so, how do we re-imagine that?

00:25:32.230 --> 00:25:34.687
I mean, that's the big question
we're tasked with today.

00:25:34.687 --> 00:25:36.870
I wonder, Shanta or Stefan,

00:25:36.870 --> 00:25:38.865
have you been in rooms, or perhaps,

00:25:38.865 --> 00:25:41.826
been leading these conversations
yourselves, in terms of,

00:25:41.826 --> 00:25:44.880
if this is not the right
blueprint moving forward,

00:25:44.880 --> 00:25:47.410
what might a better plan look like?

00:25:47.410 --> 00:25:49.390
Or, is a global plan just unrealistic,

00:25:49.390 --> 00:25:50.960
and this has to be placed by place?

00:25:50.960 --> 00:25:51.890
I think the challenge there

00:25:51.890 --> 00:25:54.740
is that doesn't really
bring the world together,

00:25:54.740 --> 00:25:56.800
which is one of the
goals of the SDGs, but-

00:25:56.800 --> 00:25:58.560
Thoughts for what re-imagining this

00:25:58.560 --> 00:25:59.440
actually might look like,

00:25:59.440 --> 00:26:01.410
or what conversations are
happening along those lines?

00:26:01.410 --> 00:26:02.900
Shanta, do you want to jump in first?

00:26:02.900 --> 00:26:04.329
- Sure.

00:26:04.329 --> 00:26:07.740
I think what we should be
having is a conversation

00:26:07.740 --> 00:26:11.590
about re-imagining aid 'cause
the real source of the problem

00:26:11.590 --> 00:26:16.590
is we are still thinking of
aid as a financial transfer.

00:26:16.970 --> 00:26:18.840
And that's, as Rory says,

00:26:18.840 --> 00:26:21.920
when that financial transfer
is such a small fraction

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:23.868
of the economy, as in Pakistan,

00:26:23.868 --> 00:26:27.400
they think that aid is not effective.

00:26:27.400 --> 00:26:30.790
On the other hand, think
about it, if an idea-

00:26:30.790 --> 00:26:35.210
If we generate an idea about scholarships

00:26:35.210 --> 00:26:37.240
for getting girls into school,

00:26:37.240 --> 00:26:40.760
that actually spreads
throughout the country,

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:43.040
that is an enormous contribution

00:26:43.040 --> 00:26:45.310
to Pakistan's economic development.

00:26:45.310 --> 00:26:47.230
And, it could be an idea
coming from outside,

00:26:47.230 --> 00:26:51.263
could be coming from Yale
University, for instance.

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:53.780
And we should be thinking,

00:26:53.780 --> 00:26:55.830
if that's where aid is really effective,

00:26:55.830 --> 00:26:57.410
that's where aid is needed.

00:26:57.410 --> 00:26:59.450
The trouble is there's
really not a lot of money

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:00.933
associated with that idea.

00:27:02.150 --> 00:27:05.520
So, the politicians will have trouble

00:27:08.050 --> 00:27:10.080
going and explaining that to the public,

00:27:10.080 --> 00:27:13.360
that here, we developed this idea

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:16.730
that's going to get girls
into school in Pakistan,

00:27:16.730 --> 00:27:20.090
and that's our aid contribution.

00:27:20.090 --> 00:27:22.480
So we really have to switch around,

00:27:22.480 --> 00:27:24.460
and you asked them about conversation,

00:27:24.460 --> 00:27:29.370
this was this almost my
refrain at the World Bank,

00:27:29.370 --> 00:27:31.720
especially when I was
Acting Chief Economist,

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:34.820
which is that I wanted the
President of the World Bank,

00:27:34.820 --> 00:27:36.210
whenever he gets on TV,

00:27:36.210 --> 00:27:38.010
whenever he gives his press statement,

00:27:38.010 --> 00:27:40.863
not to start with the amount
of money they have let.

00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:43.708
Let's start with-

00:27:43.708 --> 00:27:47.490
Either start with how many girls
that we gotten into school,

00:27:47.490 --> 00:27:52.450
or how many children have we
prevented from malnutrition,

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:54.500
or at the very least,

00:27:54.500 --> 00:27:57.690
what are the three or four
ideas we have generated

00:27:57.690 --> 00:27:59.450
that might get girls into school

00:27:59.450 --> 00:28:01.590
or improve child nutrition.

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:05.600
And then say, "Oh, by the
way, some of this costs money,

00:28:05.600 --> 00:28:08.130
and this is how much money
we're prepared to let."

00:28:08.130 --> 00:28:08.963
But I still-

00:28:08.963 --> 00:28:09.810
I have failed.

00:28:09.810 --> 00:28:13.600
I mean, you know, you see,
even in the COVID response,

00:28:13.600 --> 00:28:16.090
every speech David Malpass gives,

00:28:16.090 --> 00:28:19.820
starts with the $60 billion or whatever

00:28:19.820 --> 00:28:21.320
that the World Bank has pledged.

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:24.243
You know, meanwhile, there
are Africans dying of COVID,

00:28:25.210 --> 00:28:29.070
who are in some sense, the
clients of the World Bank,

00:28:29.070 --> 00:28:33.313
and this 60 billion doesn't
mean much to them at this point.

00:28:34.200 --> 00:28:35.070
- It's such a great point.

00:28:35.070 --> 00:28:37.690
And, you know, I often
think about the SDGs

00:28:37.690 --> 00:28:39.530
through the lens of
the role of journalism.

00:28:39.530 --> 00:28:41.580
And, I do feel that's part of the problem

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:43.950
in how this narrative is covered as well,

00:28:43.950 --> 00:28:46.280
is journalists jump onto the numbers,

00:28:46.280 --> 00:28:47.760
like this much was committed,

00:28:47.760 --> 00:28:48.910
but the key questions are:

00:28:48.910 --> 00:28:49.930
Where did the money go?

00:28:49.930 --> 00:28:51.590
And, what actually resulted?

00:28:51.590 --> 00:28:53.920
And, you know, weeks
after the press release,

00:28:53.920 --> 00:28:55.510
when we moved on to other stories,

00:28:55.510 --> 00:28:56.920
not following those commitments.

00:28:56.920 --> 00:29:01.210
I think that's a critical part
of actually making progress.

00:29:01.210 --> 00:29:02.470
I want to remind our audience

00:29:02.470 --> 00:29:04.260
that we really want to hear
from you all with questions.

00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:06.280
I see a few starting to come in,

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:09.770
but just go ahead and submit
that in the Q and A function.

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:12.520
And you can let me know if you
want your name to be shared,

00:29:12.520 --> 00:29:14.130
or if you prefer to be
anonymous, that's fine too,

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:16.340
but we want to bring in your voices.

00:29:16.340 --> 00:29:19.020
Stefan, I want to go to you in terms of,

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:21.320
you know, could this be a turning point?

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:25.250
And if so, where do you
hope things will lead?

00:29:25.250 --> 00:29:26.860
- Yeah, I know. So, let's-

00:29:26.860 --> 00:29:30.247
Picking up on where Rory was is that I do,

00:29:30.247 --> 00:29:33.380
you know, I do totally agree with him

00:29:33.380 --> 00:29:37.620
of this importance of
starting from place in time.

00:29:37.620 --> 00:29:40.750
And just even Catherine, you know,

00:29:40.750 --> 00:29:43.250
I just wish that journalists, maybe,

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:45.870
should stop publishing all
these big announcements

00:29:45.870 --> 00:29:48.200
and pledges that everybody
made in money terms,

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:50.540
because I definitely, in last ten years,

00:29:50.540 --> 00:29:52.220
have failed to stop my politicians.

00:29:52.220 --> 00:29:54.967
And I'm sorry, Rory, including
you, to actually (indistinct)

00:29:54.967 --> 00:29:57.100
to announce these big
numbers all the time.

00:29:57.100 --> 00:29:59.773
And it seems to be a,
yes, it is a problem.

00:30:00.790 --> 00:30:04.030
It's an endless set of announcements.

00:30:04.030 --> 00:30:06.190
You know, yesterday we
had 1 billion pledged

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:07.680
in humanitarian aid for Afghanistan.

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:08.513
I don't know what it means.

00:30:08.513 --> 00:30:10.903
And, I honestly don't know what it means.

00:30:12.450 --> 00:30:14.040
And so anyway-

00:30:14.040 --> 00:30:17.540
But starting from that, you
know, try to build this.

00:30:17.540 --> 00:30:20.108
I think, the way I would
like to think about,

00:30:20.108 --> 00:30:22.580
and in the end, because we are outside,

00:30:22.580 --> 00:30:24.690
there's a lot of what
we do is outside this.

00:30:24.690 --> 00:30:27.230
We need to, kind of, always
be conscious of that.

00:30:27.230 --> 00:30:30.550
So starting from a particular
place and time, you know,

00:30:30.550 --> 00:30:33.720
I would always start from
trying to understand,

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:36.540
and that's what I would tell
also our teams on the ground

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:39.260
to not understand the
nature of the commitment,

00:30:39.260 --> 00:30:42.270
that in that country exists for things

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:44.130
like sustainable growth and development,

00:30:44.130 --> 00:30:46.860
and poverty alleviation,
and just understand

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:49.050
where does the political elite want to go?

00:30:49.050 --> 00:30:50.990
Where is it? Where does it want to go?

00:30:50.990 --> 00:30:53.620
Now, that's and important question

00:30:53.620 --> 00:30:56.290
because we usually don't start with that.

00:30:56.290 --> 00:30:59.270
We usually think, oh, we're
going to buy some outcomes,

00:30:59.270 --> 00:31:00.103
to do some outcomes.

00:31:00.103 --> 00:31:03.240
Now, you know, spending money,
while it's really important,

00:31:03.240 --> 00:31:06.000
but actually without that understanding,

00:31:06.000 --> 00:31:08.100
we will forget properly to assess it

00:31:08.100 --> 00:31:10.550
because I think there's
always should be two tests

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:11.790
of anything that's happening.

00:31:11.790 --> 00:31:14.340
And it's whether it's public spending

00:31:14.340 --> 00:31:16.520
in the country or outside,
to bring your resources in,

00:31:16.520 --> 00:31:18.560
is that, of course, we should be keen

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:21.420
on making sure that the
money is used effectively.

00:31:21.420 --> 00:31:23.400
You know, that outcomes
are being generated.

00:31:23.400 --> 00:31:25.320
And, you know, that's a
good thing about the SDGs.

00:31:25.320 --> 00:31:26.790
At least it talks about outcomes,

00:31:26.790 --> 00:31:29.870
and it doesn't talk about
some kind of inputs.

00:31:29.870 --> 00:31:31.840
But at the same time, ask yourself:

00:31:31.840 --> 00:31:33.100
Why we do this?

00:31:33.100 --> 00:31:36.520
Is this the best way
of using your resources

00:31:36.520 --> 00:31:39.460
to create, at least, a chance

00:31:39.460 --> 00:31:40.870
that the underlying
commitment to development

00:31:40.870 --> 00:31:43.603
in the political space
will start improving?

00:31:44.470 --> 00:31:45.460
And actually, you know,

00:31:45.460 --> 00:31:47.820
when dealing with the case
that Rory says, you know,

00:31:47.820 --> 00:31:51.120
if you do it in South Sudan,
you have to ask yourself:

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:54.480
Do I give an excuse to
Salva Kiir or Machar

00:31:54.480 --> 00:31:56.683
to actually ignoring
entirely what we're doing?

00:31:56.683 --> 00:31:59.570
I remember being, actually,
once as a DIFD person,

00:31:59.570 --> 00:32:02.060
going to rebel-held territory,

00:32:02.060 --> 00:32:04.250
applauding that we were spending so much

00:32:04.250 --> 00:32:05.550
on health, education.

00:32:05.550 --> 00:32:06.457
And this guy said,

00:32:06.457 --> 00:32:09.380
"Yes, so I don't have to
care about these things."

00:32:09.380 --> 00:32:11.800
Now, that's a real issue.

00:32:11.800 --> 00:32:14.690
Now, there's other countries,
where Ethiopia a decade ago,

00:32:14.690 --> 00:32:15.523
where you could say,

00:32:15.523 --> 00:32:17.290
look, they wanted to try to do something.

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:20.035
Yes, maybe it didn't succeed
in the way we wanted it,

00:32:20.035 --> 00:32:22.380
but you can work with them in doing it.

00:32:22.380 --> 00:32:24.450
So, you have a sense of
that underlying commitment,

00:32:24.450 --> 00:32:28.820
you use your resources, not
just to buy some outcomes.

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:31.490
Of course, spend your money
well, but keep on thinking:

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:34.820
Am I nudging this country
forward in the line,

00:32:34.820 --> 00:32:37.250
in terms of an underlying
commitment to development?

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:40.663
And yeah, that's probably
how I would think about it.

00:32:41.650 --> 00:32:42.483
- Thanks, Stefan.

00:32:42.483 --> 00:32:44.070
I want to bring a question
in from our audience,

00:32:44.070 --> 00:32:46.420
unless Rory or Shanta, you
wanted to respond to that.

00:32:46.420 --> 00:32:47.672
Otherwise we're going
to go to some questions

00:32:47.672 --> 00:32:48.505
from the audience.

00:32:48.505 --> 00:32:49.970
But Rory, did you want to jump in?

00:32:49.970 --> 00:32:51.415
- Well, let me come in
very quickly. I mean-

00:32:51.415 --> 00:32:53.312
I think the other thing
to understand is this:

00:32:53.312 --> 00:32:54.550
There's always the counterfactuals.

00:32:54.550 --> 00:32:56.890
So this is the boring thing about life,

00:32:56.890 --> 00:32:59.780
is that if one imagines, in practice,

00:32:59.780 --> 00:33:03.110
what would really be involved
in saying we're not going?

00:33:03.110 --> 00:33:04.350
I mean, Stefan's of course, right.

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:07.450
I mean, there's not much
point in reinforcing failure.

00:33:07.450 --> 00:33:09.470
Obviously, there's not much
point in trying to help somebody

00:33:09.470 --> 00:33:11.380
who doesn't really want you to help them.

00:33:11.380 --> 00:33:13.880
You know, not much point
in trying to believe

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:15.260
that giving money to Salva Kiir

00:33:15.260 --> 00:33:17.170
is going to really lead to much.

00:33:17.170 --> 00:33:19.450
But the problem is that,

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:22.460
that is very, very
difficult for us to bear

00:33:24.260 --> 00:33:26.040
because what would that really mean?

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:28.280
That would effectively
say, well, actually,

00:33:28.280 --> 00:33:29.790
basically all the money we're spending

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:31.210
in South Sudan's a complete waste of time.

00:33:31.210 --> 00:33:36.210
So, what? Are we not going to
spend money on South Sudan?

00:33:36.607 --> 00:33:37.710
And let me give an example

00:33:37.710 --> 00:33:40.270
that really struck me most directly.

00:33:40.270 --> 00:33:42.120
Stefan's point, which
is a really good one,

00:33:42.120 --> 00:33:44.780
about their elites isn't
just true of national elites.

00:33:44.780 --> 00:33:46.350
It's also true in many of these countries,

00:33:46.350 --> 00:33:48.410
but it will be differences
between different local elites.

00:33:48.410 --> 00:33:50.730
So, in Afghanistan, for example,

00:33:50.730 --> 00:33:54.987
from 2001 to 2021, 3 million people

00:33:56.480 --> 00:33:58.698
in the Bamyan Province
in Central Afghanistan,

00:33:58.698 --> 00:34:01.520
were extremely keen to work
with international community.

00:34:01.520 --> 00:34:03.106
They had good local leaders,

00:34:03.106 --> 00:34:05.610
all the investment we put
in went a very long way.

00:34:05.610 --> 00:34:08.920
There was a fantastic explosion
of high quality schools.

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:10.850
As their women were going to university,

00:34:10.850 --> 00:34:13.520
there was some fantastic
economic development projects.

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:16.240
But of course, we put almost
no money into that area.

00:34:16.240 --> 00:34:17.480
Why did we put no money in that area?

00:34:17.480 --> 00:34:20.570
Because that was the
peaceful area of Afghanistan.

00:34:20.570 --> 00:34:22.900
Instead, the pressure
was to pump all the money

00:34:22.900 --> 00:34:24.870
into the insurgency areas in the south.

00:34:24.870 --> 00:34:27.580
The argument being, all these
people are trying to kill us.

00:34:27.580 --> 00:34:28.780
And the reason they're trying to kill us

00:34:28.780 --> 00:34:31.060
is because there's not
enough economic development,

00:34:31.060 --> 00:34:32.020
they're all unemployed.

00:34:32.020 --> 00:34:35.570
So, if we can just generate
some more economic activity

00:34:35.570 --> 00:34:36.600
in Kandahar and Helmand,

00:34:36.600 --> 00:34:38.630
then obviously people
won't join the Taliban.

00:34:38.630 --> 00:34:39.900
They'll love the Afghan government more,

00:34:39.900 --> 00:34:42.300
and our job will be done for us, right?

00:34:42.300 --> 00:34:45.150
Now, imagine if I had won my
argument with Hillary Clinton.

00:34:45.150 --> 00:34:47.820
I tried, you know, directly
with Secretary Clinton

00:34:47.820 --> 00:34:48.750
to make the Stefan argument.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:50.250
I said, why are we wasting our money

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:51.710
in Southern Afghanistan?

00:34:51.710 --> 00:34:54.188
'Cause nothing you're
doing is sustainable.

00:34:54.188 --> 00:34:56.410
You build a bridge, it gets blown up.

00:34:56.410 --> 00:34:58.540
Your development workers
can't visit these projects.

00:34:58.540 --> 00:35:00.100
Why don't we put the
money into the communities

00:35:00.100 --> 00:35:02.107
in the center and the north
that want to work with us?

00:35:02.107 --> 00:35:04.147
In fact, leaders in the
north are saying to me,

00:35:04.147 --> 00:35:04.980
"What do I have to do?

00:35:04.980 --> 00:35:06.610
Do I have to kill some American soldiers?

00:35:06.610 --> 00:35:07.610
Do I have to let off a bomb?

00:35:07.610 --> 00:35:10.990
And what do I have to do to get
some government assistance?"

00:35:10.990 --> 00:35:13.320
But of course, you can also
understand as I tell the story,

00:35:13.320 --> 00:35:17.520
exactly why it was impossible
for me to win that argument,

00:35:17.520 --> 00:35:19.507
and why it's almost
unbearable people to say,

00:35:19.507 --> 00:35:20.830
"Okay, we're going to turn our back

00:35:20.830 --> 00:35:23.740
on Southern Eastern Afghanistan
'cause it's too complicated,

00:35:23.740 --> 00:35:25.817
and we'll put our money
into the peaceful parts."

00:35:27.520 --> 00:35:28.353
- Thank you, Rory.

00:35:28.353 --> 00:35:30.920
I'm glad we're zooming in on
Afghanistan in particular,

00:35:30.920 --> 00:35:35.370
in terms of how it highlights
some of the big problems

00:35:35.370 --> 00:35:38.901
with sustainable development
with aid, as Shanta said.

00:35:38.901 --> 00:35:41.630
I wanna touch on a question
we got from the audience.

00:35:41.630 --> 00:35:44.993
And, I think it builds on a
point Rory was making earlier.

00:35:46.480 --> 00:35:50.650
Rory, you were talking
about how, basically it's,

00:35:50.650 --> 00:35:53.380
problems seem easier to
solve when they're far away.

00:35:53.380 --> 00:35:55.260
And, I see that all the
time in my reporting.

00:35:55.260 --> 00:35:57.130
So, for example:

00:35:57.130 --> 00:36:02.030
Many donors in the US supporting
global education efforts,

00:36:02.030 --> 00:36:03.430
and increasingly with SDGs,

00:36:03.430 --> 00:36:07.430
we're trying to capture not
just butts in seats, right?

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:11.760
Attendance, but learning actual outcomes.

00:36:11.760 --> 00:36:13.020
Shanta, I know this is something

00:36:13.020 --> 00:36:14.970
that you've written on as well,

00:36:14.970 --> 00:36:17.790
that all this money is
spent for education,

00:36:17.790 --> 00:36:20.160
and yet, you know, kids at a certain age,

00:36:20.160 --> 00:36:22.433
can't read basic sentences.

00:36:23.320 --> 00:36:25.670
And, so the question,

00:36:25.670 --> 00:36:27.840
just to sort of recap what
someone had sent in is:

00:36:27.840 --> 00:36:31.193
Essentially, if this is
a problem that, you know,

00:36:32.030 --> 00:36:36.770
people closer to these
contexts are better positioned

00:36:36.770 --> 00:36:38.010
to solve problems, right?

00:36:38.010 --> 00:36:39.090
And, I write about this a lot too.

00:36:39.090 --> 00:36:42.850
Like, local innovators are
best positioned to address,

00:36:42.850 --> 00:36:45.410
say, education challenges
in their country.

00:36:45.410 --> 00:36:47.070
And yet, that's not where the money is.

00:36:47.070 --> 00:36:49.520
That's what Shanta had
mentioned earlier, right?

00:36:49.520 --> 00:36:51.450
So I guess the question for you all is:

00:36:51.450 --> 00:36:55.990
How do we take steps when
it comes to re-imagining aid

00:36:55.990 --> 00:37:00.000
from top-down, donor
countries giving money

00:37:00.000 --> 00:37:02.970
with little oversight as to
where that money actually goes,

00:37:02.970 --> 00:37:05.750
and how it results in
real outcomes to more,

00:37:05.750 --> 00:37:06.583
as it's often described,

00:37:06.583 --> 00:37:09.580
bottom-up, locally developed solutions,

00:37:09.580 --> 00:37:13.730
working to target problems,

00:37:13.730 --> 00:37:17.063
more proximity in
problem-solving and funding.

00:37:17.970 --> 00:37:19.023
Of course, the challenge
is that's not where,

00:37:19.023 --> 00:37:20.790
really, the money lies.

00:37:20.790 --> 00:37:23.930
So, in many of the contexts
where we really need

00:37:23.930 --> 00:37:25.660
to make progress on the SDGs,

00:37:25.660 --> 00:37:26.743
if that's the useful framework,

00:37:26.743 --> 00:37:28.760
that's not necessarily
where the money exists

00:37:28.760 --> 00:37:29.593
to solve the problems.

00:37:29.593 --> 00:37:31.600
So, ultimately it does come from far away.

00:37:31.600 --> 00:37:34.393
So, thoughts on how we
change that paradigm?

00:37:37.401 --> 00:37:38.833
Go ahead, Shanta.

00:37:39.920 --> 00:37:44.023
- So let me suggest a, yet
another, outrageous idea.

00:37:45.150 --> 00:37:48.367
But it's actually been
practiced in a few places.

00:37:49.244 --> 00:37:50.920
You know, Rory mentioned, you know,

00:37:50.920 --> 00:37:55.030
Salva Kiir is not interested
in dealing with donors,

00:37:55.030 --> 00:37:57.020
but the people South Sudan

00:37:57.020 --> 00:38:00.760
are desperately in need
of some assistance,

00:38:00.760 --> 00:38:03.893
regardless of, you know,
what the leadership thinks.

00:38:05.180 --> 00:38:09.530
And if we are essentially
interested in helping the people,

00:38:09.530 --> 00:38:14.230
why not find a way to
get the aid to the people

00:38:14.230 --> 00:38:16.430
without having to go
through the government?

00:38:17.950 --> 00:38:22.870
So, the version of that is cash transfers.

00:38:22.870 --> 00:38:24.803
Universal, basic transfers.

00:38:27.120 --> 00:38:29.850
I would do the thought experiment,

00:38:29.850 --> 00:38:32.633
take the current amount of
aid going to any country,

00:38:33.620 --> 00:38:34.907
and convert it into cash transfers

00:38:34.907 --> 00:38:37.007
that goes to every citizen of the country,

00:38:38.610 --> 00:38:41.250
and ask, which of those two models

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:44.970
is going to generate more development.

00:38:44.970 --> 00:38:48.670
And I think, I said, this sounds radical.

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:51.990
Although it's been proposed
by many people over the years,

00:38:51.990 --> 00:38:56.840
but I think the USAID is now
introduced this as a benchmark.

00:38:56.840 --> 00:38:57.770
You know, they say,

00:38:57.770 --> 00:39:00.660
whenever you do a project, you
have to answer the question:

00:39:00.660 --> 00:39:03.416
Would it be better to take
this money and give it out

00:39:03.416 --> 00:39:08.416
as a pure cash transfer,
simply as a technical exercise.

00:39:10.060 --> 00:39:12.950
But, I suspect that in real life,

00:39:12.950 --> 00:39:14.803
we would get a lot more development.

00:39:15.940 --> 00:39:18.510
We have a lot of unemployed bureaucrats

00:39:18.510 --> 00:39:21.250
and World Bank officials.

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:26.250
But nevertheless, we can get
more development this way

00:39:27.430 --> 00:39:28.903
and it may be worth trying.

00:39:30.480 --> 00:39:31.783
- I'm really glad you
brought up cash transfers.

00:39:31.783 --> 00:39:34.430
And I want to stay on
that topic for a moment

00:39:34.430 --> 00:39:38.320
'cause when we talk about
ideas to re-imagine aid,

00:39:38.320 --> 00:39:42.050
you are absolutely right
that cash transfers,

00:39:42.050 --> 00:39:43.980
when initially suggested,

00:39:43.980 --> 00:39:46.540
seems like a crazy idea
to a lot of people.

00:39:46.540 --> 00:39:49.570
And yet, now it's really
becoming a benchmark

00:39:49.570 --> 00:39:50.403
for donor agencies.

00:39:50.403 --> 00:39:51.600
And I'll throw out there,

00:39:51.600 --> 00:39:53.890
just in terms of how
quickly this has happened.

00:39:53.890 --> 00:39:57.570
So, I started covering tech and innovation

00:39:57.570 --> 00:40:00.820
in global development,
coming up on seven years ago,

00:40:00.820 --> 00:40:04.200
and one of the groups I wrote
about was Give Directly,

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:07.420
which is really a pioneer
in cash transfers.

00:40:07.420 --> 00:40:09.870
And Give Directly had
this revolutionary idea

00:40:09.870 --> 00:40:13.380
to just give people cash and
see what they do with it.

00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:18.380
And, I think part of why it
didn't initially take off,

00:40:18.790 --> 00:40:21.100
or there was some discomfort

00:40:21.100 --> 00:40:22.530
in the global development community,

00:40:22.530 --> 00:40:25.250
is it leads people to ask:

00:40:25.250 --> 00:40:26.470
Why not cash

00:40:26.470 --> 00:40:28.650
for all the different
programs they're implementing?

00:40:28.650 --> 00:40:31.940
If cash becomes the benchmark,
and what they are doing,

00:40:31.940 --> 00:40:35.500
their livelihoods at
development agencies, at NGOs,

00:40:35.500 --> 00:40:38.740
isn't performing better than
just giving people money,

00:40:38.740 --> 00:40:42.410
you know, that may
leave them out of a job.

00:40:42.410 --> 00:40:46.460
So, I think it was quite
disruptive, quite controversial.

00:40:46.460 --> 00:40:48.420
And I would just throw out there,

00:40:48.420 --> 00:40:50.690
as someone who covers tech
and innovation in this space,

00:40:50.690 --> 00:40:53.220
I think part of the challenge
with re-imagining aid,

00:40:53.220 --> 00:40:56.980
is there's a whole industry
built up around foreign aid,

00:40:56.980 --> 00:40:59.550
global development, and jobs at stake.

00:40:59.550 --> 00:41:02.860
And, you know, people
don't necessarily want

00:41:02.860 --> 00:41:06.040
to address the status quo

00:41:06.040 --> 00:41:08.500
if it's not in their
personal, best interests.

00:41:08.500 --> 00:41:10.047
Perhaps, I'm being a
little controversial here,

00:41:10.047 --> 00:41:14.350
but I know we want to generate
some real dialogue and debate

00:41:14.350 --> 00:41:15.630
in this series.

00:41:15.630 --> 00:41:20.330
And yet, you know, there's no
better time than now it seems,

00:41:20.330 --> 00:41:22.070
given the crises that we've mentioned,

00:41:22.070 --> 00:41:24.760
to really take a hard look
at how this industry works

00:41:24.760 --> 00:41:25.720
and how to make it work better.

00:41:25.720 --> 00:41:27.853
So, sticking on cash transfers,

00:41:29.130 --> 00:41:30.420
whether we want to talk
about cash transfers

00:41:30.420 --> 00:41:32.840
or other disruptive ideas and
why they haven't taken hold,

00:41:32.840 --> 00:41:35.503
I think Rory and Stefan
wanna jump in on that.

00:41:35.503 --> 00:41:37.913
- I mean, cash transfer's
a big, big subject.

00:41:39.338 --> 00:41:43.600
I think it's true, of course.

00:41:43.600 --> 00:41:46.610
Shanta says that there are
many development programs

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:48.630
that are so useless
that you would be better

00:41:48.630 --> 00:41:50.210
at dropping cash out of a helicopter

00:41:50.210 --> 00:41:52.420
than bothering to do
it in the first place.

00:41:52.420 --> 00:41:53.350
And indeed, many projects,

00:41:53.350 --> 00:41:54.990
where we spend hundreds of millions,

00:41:54.990 --> 00:41:56.340
and achieve absolutely nothing.

00:41:56.340 --> 00:41:57.173
That's for sure.

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:01.450
I think there is a distinction to be made

00:42:01.450 --> 00:42:02.530
within cash transfers.

00:42:02.530 --> 00:42:04.870
I think cash transfers in
the humanitarian context

00:42:04.870 --> 00:42:06.020
makes an enormous amount of sense,

00:42:06.020 --> 00:42:08.370
makes a huge amount of sense to say,

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:10.550
rather than the United
States shipping over wheat

00:42:10.550 --> 00:42:13.277
from the United States, you
give somebody some money

00:42:13.277 --> 00:42:16.060
and they go and buy what
they actually want or need,

00:42:16.060 --> 00:42:17.100
which might be a tenth.

00:42:17.100 --> 00:42:19.283
Why make something else
in the local market?

00:42:20.210 --> 00:42:23.240
I think cash transfers also
make an enormous amount of sense

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:27.710
when it is a larger cash
transfer as a one-off payment.

00:42:27.710 --> 00:42:30.100
If you were, for example, to give $250

00:42:30.100 --> 00:42:34.020
to a poor woman in a
community in Bangladesh,

00:42:34.020 --> 00:42:36.980
and it's allowed her
to buy some livestock,

00:42:36.980 --> 00:42:39.083
or set up a small shop,

00:42:39.950 --> 00:42:43.130
there's good credible evidence
that over a year or two,

00:42:43.130 --> 00:42:44.050
she might be able

00:42:44.050 --> 00:42:46.773
to significantly improve
the savings and investment.

00:42:48.171 --> 00:42:53.171
However, I am much more
skeptical about the idea of,

00:42:54.310 --> 00:42:55.143
essentially trying

00:42:55.143 --> 00:42:57.973
to run other people's
social welfare systems.

00:42:59.230 --> 00:43:00.730
And there are two reasons for that, right?

00:43:00.730 --> 00:43:01.805
So the third-

00:43:01.805 --> 00:43:02.638
One's humanitarian.

00:43:02.638 --> 00:43:03.840
Second is trying to set
up the small business.

00:43:03.840 --> 00:43:06.790
The third is basically saying, here are,

00:43:06.790 --> 00:43:11.790
for example, in Pakistan, elderly
people or disabled people,

00:43:12.040 --> 00:43:14.520
and we are simply gonna fund
the Pakistan government,

00:43:14.520 --> 00:43:17.420
giving them $30 a month, right?

00:43:17.420 --> 00:43:18.730
What is the problem with that?

00:43:18.730 --> 00:43:20.530
The first problem is one of scale.

00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:23.447
The economies of the developing world

00:43:23.447 --> 00:43:24.950
are now roughly the same size

00:43:24.950 --> 00:43:27.030
as the economies of the developed world.

00:43:27.030 --> 00:43:30.070
And, even if we spent 0.7% of our GDP

00:43:30.070 --> 00:43:31.300
in the developing world.

00:43:31.300 --> 00:43:33.480
On the developed world
on developing world,

00:43:33.480 --> 00:43:36.860
we'd only be contributing
to 0.7% towards their GDPs.

00:43:36.860 --> 00:43:39.730
We couldn't begin to provide
the kind of cash required

00:43:39.730 --> 00:43:41.630
to run social welfare systems.

00:43:41.630 --> 00:43:43.710
But there's a more fundamental problem,

00:43:43.710 --> 00:43:47.400
which is, I think, it would
be politically reckless.

00:43:47.400 --> 00:43:50.020
It would actually destroy public support

00:43:50.020 --> 00:43:52.470
for international development
programs over time.

00:43:53.320 --> 00:43:55.140
Because you are effectively saying

00:43:55.140 --> 00:43:57.100
to a grandmother in Arkansas,

00:43:57.100 --> 00:43:59.330
I'm going to take $20 out
of your pocket this month,

00:43:59.330 --> 00:44:00.290
and I'm going to put it directly

00:44:00.290 --> 00:44:02.170
in the pocket of somebody else

00:44:02.170 --> 00:44:03.460
at the other end of the world.

00:44:03.460 --> 00:44:05.860
And this is not how people
envisage development.

00:44:06.730 --> 00:44:08.790
People, if they think
about development at all,

00:44:08.790 --> 00:44:10.710
were brought up to
think this is all about,

00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:12.290
not giving somebody a fish,

00:44:12.290 --> 00:44:14.490
it's about teaching them to fish.

00:44:14.490 --> 00:44:15.750
They thought about development

00:44:15.750 --> 00:44:17.210
in terms of technical assistance.

00:44:17.210 --> 00:44:19.140
As Shanta said, bringing ideas,

00:44:19.140 --> 00:44:20.360
bring infrastructure vendors,

00:44:20.360 --> 00:44:23.310
doing productive investments
that generate much more

00:44:23.310 --> 00:44:25.890
than the dollar's return
for dollar's investment.

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:28.180
And you cannot sustain, I think,

00:44:28.180 --> 00:44:30.870
the idea that the public will get itself

00:44:30.870 --> 00:44:34.500
into the world of simply
funding, with its own cash,

00:44:34.500 --> 00:44:36.740
social welfare systems in
other people's countries,

00:44:36.740 --> 00:44:37.840
when they're struggling,

00:44:37.840 --> 00:44:39.770
they feel, to fund social welfare programs

00:44:39.770 --> 00:44:41.320
in their own countries.

00:44:41.320 --> 00:44:43.810
I think it's very dangerous
thing for people to do,

00:44:43.810 --> 00:44:45.700
even if there was evidence

00:44:45.700 --> 00:44:47.650
that these approaches were sustainable.

00:44:48.980 --> 00:44:49.813
- Smart point.

00:44:49.813 --> 00:44:50.990
Stefan, do you wanna jump in on that?

00:44:50.990 --> 00:44:52.600
- Yeah, I'm just looking.

00:44:52.600 --> 00:44:56.740
And, having lived through
what Rory is also describing

00:44:56.740 --> 00:44:58.973
in terms of, you know, the-

00:45:00.700 --> 00:45:03.123
Well, I recognize it. So, look.

00:45:03.123 --> 00:45:06.900
You know, when we talking what
is most effective as aids,

00:45:06.900 --> 00:45:08.190
I totally agree with Shanta.

00:45:08.190 --> 00:45:09.873
I mean, we have a huge amount of evidence

00:45:09.873 --> 00:45:13.280
that it's good for people, and it solves

00:45:13.280 --> 00:45:16.660
in some of those fundamentally,
defectiveness problem.

00:45:16.660 --> 00:45:19.043
I recognize what Rory describes.

00:45:19.043 --> 00:45:21.855
I would actually say, Give
Directly didn't quite invent it.

00:45:21.855 --> 00:45:24.490
I think DFID was doing this
for a long time beforehand,

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:25.883
but that's another matter.

00:45:29.539 --> 00:45:32.120
But, I think the bigger
problem I would have with it

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:35.410
is that, while it's
solves a problem for aid,

00:45:35.410 --> 00:45:38.660
it doesn't solve development and growth.

00:45:38.660 --> 00:45:43.660
And now, it is possible
that we want to be skeptical

00:45:45.430 --> 00:45:50.420
about outside is able to
influence development and growth,

00:45:50.420 --> 00:45:52.407
and therefore go for
these aid's effectiveness.

00:45:52.407 --> 00:45:54.280
And that's maybe the South Sudan example.

00:45:54.280 --> 00:45:55.347
It's not a bad one.

00:45:56.960 --> 00:45:58.920
I probably am still more ambitious,

00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:00.340
what you want to be with aid.

00:46:00.340 --> 00:46:03.197
You know, I don't want to be
as gloomy as (indistinct).

00:46:04.390 --> 00:46:06.800
And also, kind of, that vision.

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:10.570
So you want to try it.
So, I'm very supportive.

00:46:10.570 --> 00:46:13.283
I would also say, look, you know,

00:46:13.283 --> 00:46:15.106
we haven't reached the end

00:46:15.106 --> 00:46:19.250
to also trying out and doing
better with cash transfers

00:46:19.250 --> 00:46:21.510
because Rory mentioned humanitarian.

00:46:21.510 --> 00:46:24.400
It's still a massive battle
to get humanitarian agencies

00:46:24.400 --> 00:46:26.478
to be willing to use cash.

00:46:26.478 --> 00:46:29.760
And, it's amazing things you can do.

00:46:29.760 --> 00:46:32.290
Just, you know, we were
recently involved in Bangladesh

00:46:32.290 --> 00:46:34.440
in a fascinating thing
with cash transfers,

00:46:34.440 --> 00:46:36.640
where we managed to get cash transfers

00:46:36.640 --> 00:46:39.960
in the pockets of people,
five days before the floods,

00:46:39.960 --> 00:46:42.480
peaked during a floods crisis,

00:46:42.480 --> 00:46:45.270
because we could use forecast
mobiles on the floods,

00:46:45.270 --> 00:46:47.750
and we basically managed to give them cash

00:46:47.750 --> 00:46:49.390
before the crisis hit.

00:46:49.390 --> 00:46:51.440
You know, you can do all kinds of things.

00:46:51.440 --> 00:46:54.240
But, all these things
are very much outsiders.

00:46:54.240 --> 00:46:55.340
I'm still a strong believer

00:46:55.340 --> 00:46:57.470
in the end development has to be,

00:46:57.470 --> 00:47:00.690
there has to be societal
ownership of development.

00:47:00.690 --> 00:47:02.990
And this is, unfortunately, not saying,

00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:05.300
well, we don't have an answer to this.

00:47:05.300 --> 00:47:08.000
I will need to still find something to do.

00:47:08.000 --> 00:47:09.630
Because we shouldn't
forget, and it comes back,

00:47:09.630 --> 00:47:11.930
actually, to a point
that Shanta made earlier.

00:47:12.980 --> 00:47:13.870
A lot of-

00:47:13.870 --> 00:47:16.530
Well, most of the resources in development

00:47:16.530 --> 00:47:18.410
are actually the government's resources.

00:47:18.410 --> 00:47:22.450
In education, it's more
than 90% of the resources

00:47:22.450 --> 00:47:25.490
in low and middle-income countries

00:47:25.490 --> 00:47:28.170
that actually will be the
government's own resources.

00:47:28.170 --> 00:47:30.507
Finding ways of getting them used well

00:47:30.507 --> 00:47:32.290
for the benefit of people,

00:47:32.290 --> 00:47:35.357
I think that's still a big
task of us of trying to see.

00:47:35.357 --> 00:47:37.460
Can we help to lock this?

00:47:37.460 --> 00:47:40.240
Cash transfers will give
us aid effectiveness,

00:47:40.240 --> 00:47:42.757
but it will not give us
development and growth.

00:47:44.930 --> 00:47:46.272
- Go ahead, Shanta. You want to jump in?

00:47:46.272 --> 00:47:47.105
- Yeah.

00:47:47.105 --> 00:47:48.320
So this is a perfect segue

00:47:48.320 --> 00:47:52.130
because I wanted to pick up
on Rory's very valid point,

00:47:52.130 --> 00:47:55.000
that if you go with aid as cash transfers,

00:47:55.000 --> 00:47:57.390
that's still a drop in the bucket

00:47:57.390 --> 00:48:01.320
in terms of the large
countries, and so on.

00:48:01.320 --> 00:48:03.400
But really, this is comes back

00:48:03.400 --> 00:48:05.303
to the question of an idea.

00:48:06.310 --> 00:48:08.270
Why not use cash transfers

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:10.483
for the government's own programs?

00:48:10.483 --> 00:48:13.020
I mean, I did an calculation-

00:48:13.020 --> 00:48:15.540
Colleagues of mine and I did a calculation

00:48:15.540 --> 00:48:18.560
about the oil-rich countries in Africa.

00:48:18.560 --> 00:48:22.160
These are, you know,
Nigeria, Gabon, Angola.

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:24.700
They're all very wealthy in terms of oil

00:48:24.700 --> 00:48:27.250
and very poor in terms of development.

00:48:27.250 --> 00:48:29.590
And we did a calculation that said,

00:48:29.590 --> 00:48:34.040
suppose you took 20% of their oil revenues

00:48:34.040 --> 00:48:37.340
and handed it out as
cash transfers equally

00:48:37.340 --> 00:48:38.703
to the entire population,

00:48:39.550 --> 00:48:43.410
in this low population
countries, like Gabon, Angola.

00:48:43.410 --> 00:48:45.810
Yeah, to some extent,
Angola, Equatorial Guinea,

00:48:46.700 --> 00:48:48.830
you could eliminate poverty.

00:48:48.830 --> 00:48:53.830
You could achieve goal
one tomorrow with that.

00:48:54.270 --> 00:48:56.430
That shows you the extent

00:48:56.430 --> 00:49:00.550
of mis-spending of public resources.

00:49:00.550 --> 00:49:01.440
And then the question,

00:49:01.440 --> 00:49:03.810
and I think Rory was alluding to this

00:49:03.810 --> 00:49:06.640
about the political support for aid,

00:49:06.640 --> 00:49:07.853
the real question is:

00:49:08.890 --> 00:49:12.340
Of course, development is not
just about people having money

00:49:12.340 --> 00:49:14.780
to spend on food, and
clothing, and things like that.

00:49:14.780 --> 00:49:16.840
It's all about public goods.

00:49:16.840 --> 00:49:19.902
And presumably, that's
why governments exist

00:49:19.902 --> 00:49:23.450
to actually provide those public goods.

00:49:23.450 --> 00:49:25.630
But the truth is that in many countries,

00:49:25.630 --> 00:49:27.070
many low and middle income countries,

00:49:27.070 --> 00:49:28.770
the governments are not
providing public goods.

00:49:28.770 --> 00:49:31.170
They're stealing the money, right?

00:49:31.170 --> 00:49:35.300
And the reason for it is that
in the resource-rich countries

00:49:35.300 --> 00:49:38.400
and in the aid-dependent
countries, I might add,

00:49:38.400 --> 00:49:41.510
the governments are not
accountable for their spending

00:49:42.430 --> 00:49:44.320
because they're not coming from taxpayers

00:49:44.320 --> 00:49:45.230
in those countries.

00:49:45.230 --> 00:49:47.170
They're coming from
taxpayers and other countries

00:49:47.170 --> 00:49:50.950
or oil consumers in other countries.

00:49:50.950 --> 00:49:54.720
So, you could think of a
situation where, let's say,

00:49:54.720 --> 00:49:58.700
a resource-rich country
hands over all the revenues

00:49:58.700 --> 00:50:01.160
as cash transfers to the citizens,

00:50:01.160 --> 00:50:05.710
and then taxes them to
finance public goods.

00:50:05.710 --> 00:50:06.830
And we can show, I mean,

00:50:06.830 --> 00:50:08.990
I've been doing some
research on this recently,

00:50:08.990 --> 00:50:10.750
can show that that actually can lead

00:50:10.750 --> 00:50:14.110
to greater accountability
by the governments,

00:50:14.110 --> 00:50:17.593
than what you have now, which
is really no accountability.

00:50:19.540 --> 00:50:20.390
- Go ahead, Rory.

00:50:22.000 --> 00:50:23.713
I think this is all fascinating.

00:50:23.713 --> 00:50:27.060
I mean, I just keep encouraging
people to try to do,

00:50:27.060 --> 00:50:28.370
because Stefan and I went through this,

00:50:28.370 --> 00:50:29.470
very painfully, with DIFD,

00:50:29.470 --> 00:50:34.470
where we found ourselves dealing
with an incredible buildup

00:50:34.650 --> 00:50:37.050
of particularly conservative
political opposition

00:50:37.050 --> 00:50:38.980
to the 0.7% that we were spending,

00:50:38.980 --> 00:50:40.870
that eventually led to the
merge in the department,

00:50:40.870 --> 00:50:42.910
and cutting of our budget.

00:50:42.910 --> 00:50:45.750
And one of the things that going on,

00:50:45.750 --> 00:50:47.220
one of the reasons people are not aware

00:50:47.220 --> 00:50:49.640
that DIFD was doing this
stuff before Give Directly

00:50:49.640 --> 00:50:52.093
is because DIFD did it almost in secret.

00:50:53.020 --> 00:50:56.040
It's very, very difficult to
find very much information

00:50:56.040 --> 00:50:58.530
about what DIFD was doing on
cash transfers on the web.

00:50:58.530 --> 00:51:01.030
I went very carefully through their

00:51:01.030 --> 00:51:02.570
testimony parliamentary committees,

00:51:02.570 --> 00:51:05.100
through their strategic plans,
through their statements.

00:51:05.100 --> 00:51:08.860
Because at some level, subconsciously,

00:51:08.860 --> 00:51:12.140
DIFD officials must've sensed
that what they were doing

00:51:12.140 --> 00:51:14.230
was not something that was
going to be very popular

00:51:14.230 --> 00:51:16.270
with the public, and that
they would find very difficult

00:51:16.270 --> 00:51:17.620
to sell to the public, right?

00:51:17.620 --> 00:51:20.007
Now, it's very easy
for technocrats to say,

00:51:20.007 --> 00:51:21.770
"Well, that's fine. We've
got the right answer.

00:51:21.770 --> 00:51:23.130
We've done the right research.

00:51:23.130 --> 00:51:24.080
This is the right thing to do.

00:51:24.080 --> 00:51:25.880
And foolish, populist public,

00:51:25.880 --> 00:51:27.000
they don't understand what we're doing.

00:51:27.000 --> 00:51:27.960
So we'll just keep doing it.

00:51:27.960 --> 00:51:29.770
And we'll just not tell you about it."

00:51:29.770 --> 00:51:33.150
But, it doesn't work. It blows
up your face, eventually.

00:51:33.150 --> 00:51:36.745
You need to have projects
that the public can relate to.

00:51:36.745 --> 00:51:37.578
And this too,

00:51:37.578 --> 00:51:38.723
I think is the problem that
Shanta's wonderful idea,

00:51:38.723 --> 00:51:41.660
that we give all the
money in cash transfers

00:51:41.660 --> 00:51:43.010
to people in Gabon,

00:51:43.010 --> 00:51:45.550
and the government of Gabon
then taxes those people

00:51:45.550 --> 00:51:47.070
and puts the money into the Gabon budget,

00:51:47.070 --> 00:51:49.010
and then spends it out.

00:51:49.010 --> 00:51:53.200
I really doubt that you
are going to be able to say

00:51:53.200 --> 00:51:54.137
to the British taxpayer,

00:51:54.137 --> 00:51:57.483
"We are going to take 10
billion pounds a year off you,

00:51:58.490 --> 00:52:00.700
and the way we're going to
spend it is going to hand it out

00:52:00.700 --> 00:52:02.320
in cash to all these people in Gabon.

00:52:02.320 --> 00:52:04.542
And then, the Gabon government

00:52:04.542 --> 00:52:06.290
is going to tax our cash and put it back.

00:52:06.290 --> 00:52:08.950
And this Gabon president,
who we might not approve of,

00:52:08.950 --> 00:52:10.620
is then going to have discretion
about how to spend it."

00:52:10.620 --> 00:52:11.876
Right?

00:52:11.876 --> 00:52:14.620
So, the problem underlying the whole thing

00:52:14.620 --> 00:52:17.423
is that, understandably,

00:52:18.350 --> 00:52:20.260
there's a culture that assumes

00:52:20.260 --> 00:52:25.110
that if research economists
come up with the right model,

00:52:25.110 --> 00:52:28.330
we can somehow trick the
public in wealthy countries

00:52:28.330 --> 00:52:29.450
into providing them money

00:52:29.450 --> 00:52:31.650
in order to follow
through on these schemes.

00:52:32.580 --> 00:52:33.920
But, we can't.

00:52:33.920 --> 00:52:38.920
Because we are failing to
understand the type of emotional,

00:52:39.450 --> 00:52:42.450
empathetic arguments that you
would actually need to make

00:52:42.450 --> 00:52:45.273
to the public to actually
believe in what's happening.

00:52:46.390 --> 00:52:48.900
And, that's also why
certain kinds of things,

00:52:48.900 --> 00:52:50.050
which we stopped doing,

00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:51.710
we may need to think about doing again.

00:52:51.710 --> 00:52:54.210
So, it's extremely
unfashionable, for example,

00:52:54.210 --> 00:52:56.693
and it has been probably since the 1950s,

00:52:57.980 --> 00:53:00.982
for internationals to try some
run high quality institutions

00:53:00.982 --> 00:53:02.770
in other people's countries.

00:53:02.770 --> 00:53:03.650
It's possible to do.

00:53:03.650 --> 00:53:05.640
Like, the Aga Khan Development Network

00:53:05.640 --> 00:53:08.320
runs an extremely good nursing
school in East Africa, right?

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:10.647
You can find examples of doing this.

00:53:10.647 --> 00:53:14.670
And my instinct is that you
could sell to Swedish voters,

00:53:14.670 --> 00:53:17.490
Swiss voters, British
voters, American voters.

00:53:17.490 --> 00:53:19.830
We are running a
fantastic university here.

00:53:19.830 --> 00:53:22.350
We're running a fantastic hospital here.

00:53:22.350 --> 00:53:25.310
But it's profoundly offensive
to development economists.

00:53:25.310 --> 00:53:27.710
It's profoundly offensive
to the academic world.

00:53:29.070 --> 00:53:32.490
And one of the problems
that I had, as a politician,

00:53:32.490 --> 00:53:35.320
is trying to get people to be practical

00:53:35.320 --> 00:53:37.810
about what kind of things
you should do on the ground

00:53:37.810 --> 00:53:40.950
that would actually excite
the domestic public,

00:53:40.950 --> 00:53:42.730
make them feel this is
something to look at,

00:53:42.730 --> 00:53:43.920
something they could be proud of,

00:53:43.920 --> 00:53:46.780
something tangible that they can see.

00:53:46.780 --> 00:53:49.640
And, I think, we fool
ourselves into thinking

00:53:49.640 --> 00:53:52.763
that we can sell things that
are fundamentally unsellable.

00:53:55.030 --> 00:53:56.060
- Thank you, Rory. Well put.

00:53:56.060 --> 00:53:57.500
And we just have a few minutes left.

00:53:57.500 --> 00:53:59.270
I can't believe that this
conversation is flown by,

00:53:59.270 --> 00:54:01.820
but I want to come back
to each of you because,

00:54:01.820 --> 00:54:05.470
in terms of the "why now"
of this conversation.

00:54:05.470 --> 00:54:06.490
One key reason,

00:54:06.490 --> 00:54:08.570
in terms of why to have
this conversation now is,

00:54:08.570 --> 00:54:11.260
as I mentioned at the outset,
the UN General Assembly.

00:54:11.260 --> 00:54:13.640
So, it kicks off today.

00:54:13.640 --> 00:54:16.030
There will be several weeks.

00:54:16.030 --> 00:54:18.270
Next week is kind of the key week.

00:54:18.270 --> 00:54:21.590
But, several weeks of big conversations

00:54:21.590 --> 00:54:24.180
that sort of take the
SDGs as a given, right?

00:54:24.180 --> 00:54:25.940
Like the SDGs as a starting point,

00:54:25.940 --> 00:54:28.650
and then talking about how have we done,

00:54:28.650 --> 00:54:31.010
how can we, you know, get to zero,

00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:33.530
achieve these goals by 2030, et cetera.

00:54:33.530 --> 00:54:36.940
I think it's really valuable
to create this space,

00:54:36.940 --> 00:54:39.490
to challenge the whole notion of the SDGs

00:54:39.490 --> 00:54:42.620
and talk about re-imagining
sustainable development

00:54:42.620 --> 00:54:43.453
and re-imagining aid.

00:54:43.453 --> 00:54:45.710
So thank you, Rory and our panelists,

00:54:45.710 --> 00:54:46.950
for sharing your insights on this.

00:54:46.950 --> 00:54:48.670
But before we close,

00:54:48.670 --> 00:54:52.670
given that the UN General
Assembly is now underway,

00:54:52.670 --> 00:54:55.200
I would love to just go
back to each of you in here.

00:54:55.200 --> 00:54:58.370
If you could gather all these leaders

00:54:58.370 --> 00:55:01.140
who are coming together
to discuss progress

00:55:01.140 --> 00:55:04.120
on the goals, with 2030 coming up,

00:55:04.120 --> 00:55:06.290
and lots of challenges posed to progress.

00:55:06.290 --> 00:55:09.490
In fact, we've actually
regressed on a lot of these goals

00:55:09.490 --> 00:55:11.350
over the course of the pandemic.

00:55:11.350 --> 00:55:13.150
What would your key message be?

00:55:13.150 --> 00:55:16.330
And, perhaps, we'll start
with Shanta and Stefan

00:55:16.330 --> 00:55:17.210
and then close with Rory.

00:55:17.210 --> 00:55:19.350
But Shanta, can I start with you?

00:55:19.350 --> 00:55:20.183
- Okay.

00:55:21.220 --> 00:55:23.570
My key message to the leaders

00:55:23.570 --> 00:55:26.460
gathering at the UN General Assembly

00:55:26.460 --> 00:55:30.200
is actually derived from our
conversation here, which is:

00:55:30.200 --> 00:55:33.963
If you want to achieve
progress on the SDGs,

00:55:35.200 --> 00:55:38.633
you have to be more
accountable to your own people.

00:55:39.780 --> 00:55:42.140
That's where you are failing,

00:55:42.140 --> 00:55:43.960
and I'll be very blunt about it.

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:46.210
Now, as I was saying earlier,

00:55:46.210 --> 00:55:47.680
I think the aid establishment

00:55:47.680 --> 00:55:50.960
may have contributed to
this lack of accountability,

00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:52.520
or weakening of accountability.

00:55:52.520 --> 00:55:56.673
So we have to do our part
as well, but you know,

00:55:58.450 --> 00:56:01.520
this is one criticism I have of the SDGs,

00:56:01.520 --> 00:56:05.660
is that we have 17 goals,
but there's one goal,

00:56:05.660 --> 00:56:09.290
I think it's goal 16, which
is the one on governance.

00:56:09.290 --> 00:56:10.813
That is primordial.

00:56:12.420 --> 00:56:13.253
That's the goal

00:56:13.253 --> 00:56:16.200
that's going to generate
all the other goals,

00:56:16.200 --> 00:56:20.002
but since it's number 16
of 17, it's easy to say,

00:56:20.002 --> 00:56:22.470
well, you know, we'll focus
on the other three or four

00:56:22.470 --> 00:56:26.693
of the other 17, and not
take seriously that one goal.

00:56:27.675 --> 00:56:30.400
And that's, that's my
message to all these leaders.

00:56:30.400 --> 00:56:32.900
- Yeah, that is absolutely
cross-cutting and foundational.

00:56:32.900 --> 00:56:33.890
Agreed.

00:56:33.890 --> 00:56:36.353
Stefan, what about you?
What would your message be?

00:56:37.680 --> 00:56:40.713
- My message would be,
potentially, it's a version of it.

00:56:40.713 --> 00:56:43.553
Although I always struggle
with the word "accountability"

00:56:43.553 --> 00:56:46.860
because it also gets interpreted
in so many different ways,

00:56:46.860 --> 00:56:48.820
but my message to them would be,

00:56:48.820 --> 00:56:52.000
is that I probably would
put them into groups.

00:56:52.000 --> 00:56:54.660
There are some countries,
in their own way,

00:56:54.660 --> 00:56:59.380
probably committed to
develop them to growth,

00:56:59.380 --> 00:57:01.540
to actually doing some of
the longer term things.

00:57:01.540 --> 00:57:05.940
And I wish I could, on
behalf of my department, say:

00:57:05.940 --> 00:57:08.050
Look, we'll support you
in that quest, you know.

00:57:08.050 --> 00:57:11.130
We allow you to gamble on development.

00:57:11.130 --> 00:57:14.240
And then, I will take that
other group there and say:

00:57:14.240 --> 00:57:17.940
Look, you know, don't expect us

00:57:17.940 --> 00:57:20.480
to keep on just trying
to compensate for you

00:57:20.480 --> 00:57:22.930
because, actually for the
reason that Rory says,

00:57:22.930 --> 00:57:26.730
we can't really permanently
overcome the fact

00:57:26.730 --> 00:57:28.490
that you're not want to do this,

00:57:28.490 --> 00:57:31.170
but if you show commitment to
doing some of these things.

00:57:31.170 --> 00:57:34.070
And maybe in Shanta's words,
if you show accountability,

00:57:34.070 --> 00:57:37.860
we will be there and we
will want to support you.

00:57:37.860 --> 00:57:42.520
But it means also, we
can't just in general, say:

00:57:42.520 --> 00:57:44.220
We'll write you a check anyway,

00:57:44.220 --> 00:57:47.070
we'll just be there as
outsiders, you know.

00:57:47.070 --> 00:57:49.040
Aid is a bit like dancing the tango.

00:57:49.040 --> 00:57:50.250
If you're not both committed,

00:57:50.250 --> 00:57:52.003
it's going to go very, very wrong.

00:57:53.330 --> 00:57:54.300
- I love that analogy.

00:57:54.300 --> 00:57:56.890
And surprisingly, had not heard
it before, but that's great.

00:57:56.890 --> 00:57:59.640
Rory, we'll close with you.
What would your message be?

00:58:00.760 --> 00:58:04.390
- I think moderation, pragmatic messaging.

00:58:04.390 --> 00:58:06.980
The biggest danger in
international development

00:58:07.960 --> 00:58:10.853
is that we produce these utopian dreams,

00:58:12.400 --> 00:58:13.410
and in doing so,

00:58:13.410 --> 00:58:16.640
we are going to inevitably
disillusion ourselves,

00:58:16.640 --> 00:58:18.470
alienate our public,

00:58:18.470 --> 00:58:21.070
destroy the credibility
of our entire project,

00:58:21.070 --> 00:58:24.810
and run the risk that we
lurch from utopian dreams

00:58:24.810 --> 00:58:28.470
to sudden extreme cutbacks, abandonment.

00:58:28.470 --> 00:58:30.520
Now, the UK government
has just brought through,

00:58:30.520 --> 00:58:32.620
in effect, 70-80% cuts

00:58:32.620 --> 00:58:35.300
in many of these fragile
conflict-affected states

00:58:35.300 --> 00:58:36.133
in its programming,

00:58:36.133 --> 00:58:37.590
because of the way it's
structured its budgets.

00:58:37.590 --> 00:58:39.770
And it ended up doing that, in part,

00:58:39.770 --> 00:58:42.250
because of this ridiculous cycle

00:58:42.250 --> 00:58:45.990
between fairy story and isolation.

00:58:45.990 --> 00:58:50.240
So, unless we can get ourselves
to framing a moderate,

00:58:50.240 --> 00:58:52.530
pragmatic way of talking
about development,

00:58:52.530 --> 00:58:54.750
that it embraces the fact

00:58:54.750 --> 00:58:58.978
that there will be partial
successes, partial failures,

00:58:58.978 --> 00:59:01.730
that things on the ground
are going to look very messy,

00:59:01.730 --> 00:59:02.840
and that we're going to partner together

00:59:02.840 --> 00:59:05.514
and listen together, in
the way that we would

00:59:05.514 --> 00:59:08.220
with difficult communities
or challenged communities

00:59:08.220 --> 00:59:10.350
in the United States or Europe,

00:59:10.350 --> 00:59:11.560
communities that we would expect

00:59:11.560 --> 00:59:13.432
to work with over 50-60 years,

00:59:13.432 --> 00:59:14.950
and don't have all the answers to,

00:59:14.950 --> 00:59:16.090
we're going to find ourselves

00:59:16.090 --> 00:59:19.200
in a hysterical, unsustainable situation.

00:59:19.200 --> 00:59:21.490
We need to keep faith and hope.

00:59:21.490 --> 00:59:24.040
And hope, above all, needs
to be rooted in realism.

00:59:26.100 --> 00:59:26.933
- Thank you, Rory.

00:59:26.933 --> 00:59:28.420
It's a powerful way to close.

00:59:28.420 --> 00:59:29.570
Probably should just end it at that.

00:59:29.570 --> 00:59:31.640
But I did want to quickly say,

00:59:31.640 --> 00:59:33.720
for those of you who are
joining us for the first time,

00:59:33.720 --> 00:59:35.770
or unaware, this is part of a series.

00:59:35.770 --> 00:59:37.530
And it's the eighth in the series

00:59:37.530 --> 00:59:39.020
of the Yale Development Dialogues.

00:59:39.020 --> 00:59:41.970
And, I'm super grateful to
moderate these conversations,

00:59:41.970 --> 00:59:44.380
bringing together insights from history,

00:59:44.380 --> 00:59:46.350
and economics and policy.

00:59:46.350 --> 00:59:49.730
It's really rare to have these
kinds of candid conversations

00:59:49.730 --> 00:59:53.590
on such critical issues,
affecting all countries, really.

00:59:53.590 --> 00:59:55.940
And I can't think of a more timely,

00:59:55.940 --> 00:59:57.500
important topic as this one.

00:59:57.500 --> 01:00:00.770
So, thank you again to
Rory, and to Shanta,

01:00:00.770 --> 01:00:02.250
and to Stefan for taking the time

01:00:02.250 --> 01:00:04.530
to tackle this big question

01:00:04.530 --> 01:00:07.791
about re-imagining sustainable
development and aid

01:00:07.791 --> 01:00:10.610
as the UN General Assembly kicks off.

01:00:10.610 --> 01:00:13.310
And stay tuned for our
upcoming conversations,

01:00:13.310 --> 01:00:16.940
tackling some other big
topics on health, on climate,

01:00:16.940 --> 01:00:19.880
and we'll conclude this fall semester,

01:00:19.880 --> 01:00:22.300
for those of you following
the academic year,

01:00:22.300 --> 01:00:25.220
we'll conclude the year with
a conversation on Afghanistan.

01:00:25.220 --> 01:00:28.113
So, thank you all. And,
we'll see you next time.

